# Whether a refusal is the MACHINE's answer, asked of the expectation, the
# case and the machine itself rather than of the compiler's words alone.
#
# `unsupported/` builds programs that bind C, so a machine that lacks what a
# case binds refuses the build in words of its own: `error[ffi_missing_header]`,
# `error[ffi_pkgconfig]`, or `pkg-config is not on this machine`. Such a case
# cannot be judged there; the author's answer *A* of 2026-10-03 skips it "there
# by name" and judges it where the library is (`.claude/rules/platforms.md`).
#
# **Until defect 246 the skip asked the compiler's words alone**
# (`shell.machine_lacks_the_library`): a refusal holding one of the three
# spellings and differing from its expectation was taken for the machine's.
# The compiler under test was the only witness to its own excuse, and measured
# on 2026-10-04 at `fd76fc45` the form read green over each of these:
#
# - a changed message in any of the 21 cases whose expectation IS such a
#   message: `136 passed, 0 failed`, the case skipped in silence;
# - a compiler saying `stdio.h` is missing on a Mac that has it, a second
#   message beside the expected one, and `pkg-config` said to be missing where
#   it runs (a stub compiler over ten matching cases: `10 passed, 0 failed`);
# - Debian's pkgconf words in the three expectations that quote `pkg-config`
#   (`quoted_here`, below).
#
# So three witnesses, and the compiler's words only name the candidate:
#
# 1. **the expectation, and the case's own `#~` marks** (`pinned`): a missing
#    library the expectation names, or a message of its class the expectation
#    or a mark places on the line it is told at, is what the case tests, so a
#    difference is the compiler's and is judged, never skipped. The marks are
#    the witness a hand that rewrites an `.expected` cannot reach (CLAUDE.md
#    § 9), and the place catches a compiler that misnames the header it did
#    not find (defect 232's class) or blames another line (226's);
# 2. **the case** (`binds`, `needs`): what is missing must be the header one of
#    its groups names, a header that header includes, or a `pkgconfig` it asks
#    for;
# 3. **the machine**, asked directly (`asked`): `pkg-config` does not start,
#    or does not know the package, or clang does not find the header on the
#    include path the compiler searches.
#
# Every suite of the net that skips for a missing library asks it, and names
# each skip on its report; the words-only predicate they shared,
# `shell.machine_lacks_the_library`, is gone, so a new suite cannot take the
# compiler's word by reaching for it. Measured before, on 2026-10-04: `lines`
# read 268 passed, 0 failed over one case a stand-in compiler said lacked
# `stdio.h`, and 269 and 0 without it.
#
# A question asked here can refuse a skip and never invent one, since the
# compiler named the absence before anything here asks: a wrong answer here is
# a red, never a green. The price is named rather than hidden: a case whose
# group reaches a header one platform lacks BEFORE the header it tests is red
# there, since the message lands where its own is pinned; ruling *A* writes
# such a case on headers of the program's own.

use <core>/strings as core_strings

use mark_readers
use shell
use strings

# One thing a refusal says this machine lacks. `kind` is `header`, `package`
# (not installed), `installed` (installed, and its flags out of `pkg-config`'s
# reach, defect 292) or `pkg-config`; `name` is the header or the package, for
# `pkg-config` the package that asked it; `owner` is, for a header another
# header includes, the header the group names, and "" otherwise. `code` is the
# diagnostic's, and "" for the sentence about `pkg-config`: the compiler tells it
# under `ffi_pkgconfig` at the package's string since defect 249, and a case
# whose expectation or mark places `ffi_pkgconfig` there pins the package's own
# message, not a machine without `pkg-config`, which the Windows box is and
# where such a case is skipped by name. `file` and `line` are where it is told,
# "" and 0 where the output does not say.
record Absence
    kind: str
    name: str
    owner: str
    code: str
    file: str
    line: i64

# THE COMPILER'S WORDS FOR A LIBRARY A MACHINE LACKS, held here and nowhere else
# in the net: each constant below is a piece one string literal of the
# compiler's source holds whole, and `SPELLINGS` names the file that holds it,
# which the test *the spellings are the compiler's own* reads. So a sentence the
# compiler rewords is a red there, naming the file and the piece, before any
# case is judged in the old words (defect 328, 2026-10-07). Until that day the
# net kept its own copies, and three had lagged in silence: `notes_of` trimmed
# pkg-config's answer before reading its `\r\n` lines, where the compiler reads
# them first since defect 285; the tests planted defect 249's old bare sentence
# about a `pkg-config` not installed; and defect 292's headline for a package
# installed whose flags pkg-config cannot give was no spelling read here at
# all, so a case over such a package read red where it is the machine's.
#
# A spelling these no longer read names no absence, so its case is judged: the
# drift is a red, never a skip. The `pkg-config` sentence joined the first two
# on 2026-08-31, when the Windows box answered it for four `run/` cases naming
# raylib and they failed as *did not build* on a machine that simply has no
# `pkg-config`.
#
# And no other module of the net spells one of them (defect 450, 2026-10-07):
# three suites wrote *clang looked and did not find it* in six places of
# their own, and `suite_golden` a group's owner, a package's close and
# pkg-config's note, each a copy defect 328's red could not see. The test
# *the compiler's words for a missing library are spelled in this module
# alone* reads every module of the net for the pieces below.
#
# The renderer writes a headline `error[<code>]: <message>` and a note
# `  note: <note>` (`selfhost/diag_render.hero`).
constant HEADER_CODE: str
    "ffi_missing_header"

constant PACKAGE_CODE: str
    "ffi_pkgconfig"

constant NOTE: str
    "  note: "

# A header no group names, reached through one that a group does (`OWNER_`),
# or named by the group itself, which clang looked for (`HEADER_LOOKED`).
constant HEADER_CLOSE: str
    "` is not on this machine's include path"

constant HEADER_LOOKED: str
    " — clang looked and did not find it"

constant OWNER_OPEN: str
    ", and `"

constant OWNER_CLOSE: str
    "`, the header this group names"

# What follows the owner's close in the compiler's words, before the line it
# includes the header at or the headers it includes it through; read by no
# absence, and spelled here so a test's plant is the compiler's sentence.
constant OWNER_TAIL: str
    ", includes it"

# A package: the headline opens with `PACKAGE_HEAD` and the rest of the
# driver's line after its marker (`PACKAGE_MARK`, whose last character opens the
# name), then one of three closes: not installed, installed with its flags
# out of reach, or `pkg-config` not on this machine at all.
constant PACKAGE_HEAD: str
    "the library "

constant PACKAGE_MARK: str
    "heroes-ffi-pkgconfig `"

constant PACKAGE_CLOSE: str
    "` is not installed on this machine"

constant INSTALLED_CLOSE: str
    "` is installed, and pkg-config cannot give its flags"

constant UNASKED_CLOSE: str
    "` cannot be looked up: "

constant TOOL_OPEN: str
    "pkg-config is not on this machine, and `pkgconfig \""

constant TOOL_CLOSE: str
    "\"` asks it where the library is"

# The notes under a package's headline: pkg-config's own words, or that it gave
# none, then the way out, which differs by headline.
constant SAID: str
    "pkg-config said: "

constant NO_REASON: str
    "pkg-config gave no reason"

constant NOT_INSTALLED_WAY: str
    "install its development files, or name the library directly with `link`"

constant INSTALLED_WAY: str
    "install what pkg-config says it lacks, or name the library directly with `link`"

constant SILENT_WAY: str
    "name the library directly with `link`"

constant TOOL_WAY: str
    "install pkg-config, or name the library directly with `link`"

# The headline openings, put together as the renderer writes them.
function header_open() -> str
    return "error[" + HEADER_CODE + "]: `"

function package_open() -> str
    return "error[" + PACKAGE_CODE + "]: " + PACKAGE_HEAD + "`"

# Where each piece stands in the compiler's source, as its literal spells it:
# a file and the text the file must hold. `heard` is the piece as the compiler
# writes it, and the test asks the file for that text as a literal writes it
# (`as_written`).
record Spelling
    file: str
    heard: str

function spellings() -> [Spelling]
    build = "selfhost/emit/ffi_build.hero"
    reach = "selfhost/emit/header_reach.hero"
    answer = "selfhost/cli/pkgconfig_answer.hero"
    return [
        Spelling(file: build, heard: "\"" + HEADER_CODE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: reach, heard: "\"" + HEADER_CODE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: build, heard: "\"" + PACKAGE_CODE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: build, heard: "\"" + PACKAGE_HEAD + "\"")
        Spelling(file: build, heard: "\"`\" + header + \"" + HEADER_CLOSE + HEADER_LOOKED + "\"")
        Spelling(file: reach, heard: "\"`\" + missing + \"" + HEADER_CLOSE + OWNER_OPEN + "\" + owner.header + \"" + OWNER_CLOSE + OWNER_TAIL + "\"")
        Spelling(file: "selfhost/emit/ffi_lookup.hero", heard: "\"" + strings.without_suffix(text: PACKAGE_MARK, suffix: "`") + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"" + PACKAGE_MARK + "\" + pkgconfig + \"" + PACKAGE_CLOSE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"" + PACKAGE_MARK + "\" + pkgconfig + \"" + INSTALLED_CLOSE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"" + PACKAGE_MARK + "\" + pkgconfig + \"" + UNASKED_CLOSE + as_written(TOOL_OPEN) + "\" + pkgconfig + \"" + as_written(TOOL_CLOSE) + "\\n  " + TOOL_WAY + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"\\n  " + SAID + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"\\n  " + NO_REASON + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "\"\\n  " + NOT_INSTALLED_WAY + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "way: str @= \"" + INSTALLED_WAY + "\"")
        Spelling(file: answer, heard: "way @ \"" + SILENT_WAY + "\"")
        Spelling(file: "selfhost/diag_render.hero", heard: "\"" + NOTE + "\"")
        Spelling(file: "selfhost/diag_render.hero", heard: "\"[\" + d.code + \"]: \"")
    ]

# `text` as a Heroes string literal spells it between its quotes: a backslash,
# a quote and a line end each escaped, as the compiler's source writes them.
function as_written(text: str) -> str
    pieces: [str] @= []
    start: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < text.len()
        byte = text[at]

        if byte == 92 || byte == 34 || byte == 10
            pieces @ pieces.push(text.slice(from: start, to: at))

            if byte == 10
                pieces @ pieces.push("\\n")
            else
                pieces @ pieces.push("\\" + text.slice(from: at, to: at + 1))
            start @ at + 1
        at @ at + 1

    pieces @ pieces.push(text.slice(from: start, to: text.len()))
    return pieces.join("")

# Whether a case's output, which differs from its expectation, is this
# MACHINE's answer, `path` being the source the compiler was given. Ok with the
# reason, naming each library, where no library the compiler says is missing
# is the case's subject (`pinned`), and each is one the case binds and the
# machine, asked, lacks. An error saying which witness refused otherwise, its
# code `none` where the compiler named nothing missing. `expected` is what the
# case expects the compiler to say, "" for a suite that expects nothing of it.
function machine_lacks(scratch: str, path: str, expected: str, said: str) -> str?
    return machine_lacks_among(scratch: scratch, path: path, modules: [path], expected: expected, said: said)

# The same, for a program of several modules: `modules` are the sources the
# caller knows the program reads, `path` first among them. Every file the
# compiler's own `at` lines blame inside `path`'s directory is read as well,
# since a group the compiler blames is where the program binds what is
# missing (`examples/ledger` binds sqlite in `db/sqlite.hero`); the driver's
# sentence about `pkg-config` names no file, so a `pkgconfig` group is found only
# in what the caller passes (the corpus passes a program's whole tree; every
# other caller its one file, which holds every `pkgconfig` group the tree has,
# the test below).
function machine_lacks_among(scratch: str, path: str, modules: [str], expected: str, said: str) -> str?
    named = absences(said)

    if named.len() == 0
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "the compiler names nothing this machine lacks")
    sources = read_sources(path: path, modules: modules, named: named)

    if sources.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unread", msg: message_of_sources(sources))
    seen = Witnesses(
        named: absences(expected),
        reported: mark_readers.reported_by(expected),
        marked: marks_of(sources.must()),
        path: path
    )

    for one in named
        if pinned(one: one, seen: seen)
            return fail(
                code: "pinned",
                msg: "the expectation names the same missing library, or it or the case's own `#~` mark places a `" + one.code + "` where this one is told, so the case tests that message and a difference in it is the compiler's"
            )

    bound = binds_of(sources.must())
    search = searched(scratch: scratch, dir: directory_of(path), bound: bound, hint: runtime_hint())

    if search.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unasked", msg: message_of_words(search))
    lacking: [str] @= []

    for one in named
        if !needs(bound: bound, one: one)
            return fail(
                code: "unbound",
                msg: "the compiler says " + shown(one) + " is missing, and " + bound_by(path: path, count: sources.must().len()) + " no such " + bound_as(one)
            )
        answer = asked(scratch: scratch, search: search.must(), one: one)

        if answer.is_err()
            return fail(
                code: "refuted",
                msg: message_of(answer) + ", so the compiler's word that " + shown(one) + " is missing is not this machine's answer"
            )
        lacking @ lacking.push(answer.must())

    return ok("this machine lacks what the case binds, so it is judged where that is: " + lacking.join("; "))

# Every absence a compiler's output names, in its order, each with the place
# it is told: the head of the brief form's line, or the `at` line under §4.17's
# headline.
function absences(text: str) -> [Absence]
    found: [Absence] @= []
    rows = strings.lines(text)
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < rows.len()
        one = absence_in(rows[at])

        if !one.is_err()
            placed: Absence @= one.must()
            here: Place @= place_of(head_of(rows[at]))

            if here.line == 0 && at + 1 < rows.len() && core_strings.has_prefix(s: rows[at + 1], prefix: "  at ")
                here @ place_of(strings.without_prefix(text: rows[at + 1], prefix: "  at "))
            placed.file @ here.file
            placed.line @ here.line
            found @ found.push(placed)
        at @ at + 1

    return found

# The absence one line names. A header's name runs to the first close after
# it, a package's to the last, since a package's name may hold a backtick
# (defect 237's case) and its line ends with the close.
function absence_in(row: str) -> Absence?
    header = between(text: row, open: header_open(), close: HEADER_CLOSE)

    if !header.is_err()
        owner = between(text: row, open: HEADER_CLOSE + OWNER_OPEN, close: OWNER_CLOSE)
        return ok(
            Absence(kind: "header", name: header.must(), owner: owner.default(""), code: HEADER_CODE, file: "", line: 0)
        )
    package = between_last(text: row, open: package_open(), close: PACKAGE_CLOSE)

    if !package.is_err()
        return ok(Absence(kind: "package", name: package.must(), owner: "", code: PACKAGE_CODE, file: "", line: 0))

    # Defect 292's headline: the package is there, and what it requires is not.
    installed = between_last(text: row, open: package_open(), close: INSTALLED_CLOSE)

    if !installed.is_err()
        return ok(Absence(kind: "installed", name: installed.must(), owner: "", code: PACKAGE_CODE, file: "", line: 0))
    asking = between_last(text: row, open: TOOL_OPEN, close: TOOL_CLOSE)

    if !asking.is_err()
        return ok(Absence(kind: "pkg-config", name: asking.must(), owner: "", code: "", file: "", line: 0))
    return fail(code: "none", msg: "this line names nothing the machine lacks")

# Where a diagnostic is told, read from `<file>:<line>:<column>`, the line 0
# where the text does not open so.
record Place
    file: str
    line: i64

function place_of(text: str) -> Place
    parts = strings.split_on(text: text, byte: 58)

    if parts.len() < 3
        return Place(file: "", line: 0)
    line = strings.to_number(parts[1])

    if line.is_err()
        return Place(file: "", line: 0)
    return Place(file: parts[0], line: line.must())

# What stands before the diagnostic's kind on a line: the brief form's
# `<file>:<line>:<column>: `, or nothing on §4.17's headline.
function head_of(row: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: "error[")

    if at.is_err()
        return ""
    return row.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

# What stands between the first `open` and the first `close` after it. Every
# `open` here ends, and every `close` begins, with an ASCII byte, so both cuts
# fall between characters.
function between(text: str, open: str, close: str) -> str?
    at = strings.index_of(text: text, needle: open)?
    rest = text.slice(from: at + open.len(), to: text.len())
    end = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: close)?
    return ok(rest.slice(from: 0, to: end))

# What stands between the first `open` and the LAST `close` after it.
function between_last(text: str, open: str, close: str) -> str?
    at = strings.index_of(text: text, needle: open)?
    rest = text.slice(from: at + open.len(), to: text.len())
    end = last_index_of(text: rest, needle: close)?
    return ok(rest.slice(from: 0, to: end))

# Where `needle` last occurs in `text`, asked of the bytes as
# `strings.index_of` asks them, or an error if it does not occur.
function last_index_of(text: str, needle: str) -> i64?
    if needle.len() == 0 || needle.len() > text.len()
        return fail(code: "absent", msg: "`" + needle + "` is not in the text")
    start: i64 @= text.len() - needle.len()

    while start >= 0
        i: i64 @= 0
        hit: bool @= true

        while i < needle.len()
            if text[start + i] != needle[i]
                hit @ false
                i @ needle.len()
            else
                i @ i + 1

        if hit
            return ok(start)
        start @ start - 1

    return fail(code: "absent", msg: "`" + needle + "` is not in the text")

# What the expectation and the case's sources say the case tests: the
# libraries the expectation names, its diagnostics as `<line>:<code>` (the
# lines of `path`), the sources' marks as `<file>:<line>:<code>`
# (`mark_readers` reads both, for this module and for the `annotations` suite,
# so there is one reader of a mark), and the path the compiler was given.
record Witnesses
    named: [Absence]
    reported: [str]
    marked: [str]
    path: str

# Whether an absence the compiler names is the case's own subject rather than
# a candidate for the machine's answer: the expectation names the same library
# (the owner not asked, since the same header blamed on another group is the
# compiler's words changing), or the expectation or a mark of the case's own
# places a diagnostic of its code on the line it is told at, which holds when
# the expectation's words are past reading and when the compiler misnames the
# library at the place the case pins.
function pinned(one: Absence, seen: Witnesses) -> bool
    for other in seen.named
        if one.kind == other.kind && one.name == other.name
            return true

    if one.code == "" || one.line == 0
        return false
    spot = one.line.to_str() + ":" + one.code

    if one.file == seen.path && listed(names: seen.reported, name: spot)
        return true
    return listed(names: seen.marked, name: one.file + ":" + spot)

# One source the witness reads: its path as the compiler spells it, its text.
record Source
    path: str
    text: str

# The sources a case's witness reads: `path`, then `modules`, then each file
# the compiler blames inside `path`'s directory, each once. One it cannot read
# fails the whole question, so the case is judged rather than excused.
function read_sources(path: str, modules: [str], named: [Absence]) -> [Source]?
    wanted: [str] @= [path]

    for one in modules
        if !listed(names: wanted, name: one)
            wanted @ wanted.push(one)

    for one in named
        if one.file != "" && inside(dir: directory_of(path), file: one.file) && !listed(names: wanted, name: one.file)
            wanted @ wanted.push(one.file)

    out: [Source] @= []

    for one in wanted
        text = read_file(one)

        if text.is_err()
            return fail(code: "unread", msg: "cannot read " + one + " to ask what it binds")
        out @ out.push(Source(path: one, text: text.must()))

    return ok(out)

# Who was read for what a case binds, as a refusal says it: the path the
# compiler was given, and how many more of the program's files beside it.
function bound_by(path: str, count: i64) -> str
    if count <= 1
        return path + " binds"

    if count == 2
        return path + " and the one other module read with it bind"
    return path + " and the " + (count - 1).to_str() + " other modules read with it bind"

# Whether `file` sits in `dir` or below it, `.` holding the bare names.
function inside(dir: str, file: str) -> bool
    if dir == "."
        return strings.index_of(text: file, needle: "/").is_err()
    return core_strings.has_prefix(s: file, prefix: dir + "/")

function message_of_sources(got: [Source]?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# Every source's marks, as `<file>:<line>:<code>`.
function marks_of(sources: [Source]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for one in sources
        for mark in mark_readers.claimed_by(one.text)
            out @ out.push(one.path + ":" + mark)

    return out

# What the sources bind together.
function binds_of(sources: [Source]) -> Binds
    headers: [str] @= []
    packages: [str] @= []

    for one in sources
        bound = binds(one.text)

        for header in bound.headers
            headers @ headers.push(header)

        for package in bound.packages
            packages @ packages.push(package)

    return Binds(headers: headers, packages: packages)

# Why a skip was refused, for the failure its caller reports: "" where the
# compiler named nothing missing at all, which is an ordinary failure and needs
# no word more.
function refused(lacks: str?) -> str
    code = match lacks
        .ok _  => "none"
        .err e => e.code

    if code == "none"
        return ""
    return message_of(lacks)

# What a case binds: the header of each `extern` group, and each group's
# `pkgconfig` name, read from its source.
record Binds
    headers: [str]
    packages: [str]

constant PACKAGE_WORD: str
    " pkgconfig "

function binds(source: str) -> Binds
    headers: [str] @= []
    packages: [str] @= []

    for row in strings.lines(source)
        if !core_strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: "extern ")
            continue
        rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "extern ")
        header = quoted(rest)

        if header.is_err()
            continue
        headers @ headers.push(header.must().value)
        tail = rest.slice(from: header.must().after, to: rest.len())
        at = strings.index_of(text: tail, needle: PACKAGE_WORD)

        if !at.is_err()
            package = quoted(tail.slice(from: at.must() + PACKAGE_WORD.len(), to: tail.len()))

            if !package.is_err()
                packages @ packages.push(package.must().value)

    return Binds(headers: headers, packages: packages)

# A string literal opening `text`: its value, `\"` and `\\` read as the byte
# each stands for, and the offset just past its closing quote. Any other escape
# stays as written, so a header named with one matches no message and its case
# is judged.
record Quoted
    value: str
    after: i64

function quoted(text: str) -> Quoted?
    if text.len() == 0 || text[0] != 34
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "no string here")
    parts: [str] @= []
    start: i64 @= 1
    at: i64 @= 1

    while at < text.len()
        if text[at] == 92 && at + 1 < text.len() && (text[at + 1] == 34 || text[at + 1] == 92)
            parts @ parts.push(text.slice(from: start, to: at))
            start @ at + 1
            at @ at + 2
        else if text[at] == 34
            parts @ parts.push(text.slice(from: start, to: at))
            return ok(Quoted(value: parts.join(""), after: at + 1))
        else
            at @ at + 1

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "a string nothing closes")

# Whether the case binds what an absence names: the header its group names, a
# header that header includes, or the package that asked.
function needs(bound: Binds, one: Absence) -> bool
    if one.kind == "header" && one.owner != ""
        return listed(names: bound.headers, name: one.owner)

    if one.kind == "header"
        return listed(names: bound.headers, name: one.name)
    return listed(names: bound.packages, name: one.name)

function listed(names: [str], name: str) -> bool
    for one in names
        if one == name
            return true

    return false

# The words clang searches with to answer for a header, as the compiler's own
# search reaches them (`selfhost/cli/compiling.hero`'s `search_words`): the
# runtime's directory first (defect 2324), each package's compile words and
# the case's own. The `--include` directories are the one part left out, since
# the harness passes none; a narrower search could only fail to refute an
# absence the compiler named, never name one. None of the compiler's own
# flags moves the search (`selfhost/cli/flags.hero`, read 2026-10-04: no
# `-isystem`, no `--target`).
# `hint` is `HEROES_RUNTIME` as `runtime_hint` reads it.
function searched(scratch: str, dir: str, bound: Binds, hint: str?) -> [str]?
    runtime: str @= hint?

    if runtime.len() == 0
        runtime @ "runtime"
    out: [str] @= ["-I", runtime]

    for package in bound.packages
        ran = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--cflags", "--", package])

        if !ran.is_err() && ran.must().started && ran.must().code == 0
            for word in strings.words(ran.must().out)
                out @ out.push(word)

    out @ out.push("-I")
    out @ out.push(dir)
    return ok(out)

# The runtime's directory as `HEROES_RUNTIME` names it, "" where it is unset,
# and a refusal where it is set and not UTF-8 (defect 243). Such a value names
# a directory no `str` can hold, so clang cannot be asked about it, and the
# compiler refuses to build under it; until 2026-10-04 it read as unset and
# clang searched `runtime` in its place, a witness about another directory.
function runtime_hint() -> str?
    hint = shell.env(name: "HEROES_RUNTIME")

    if hint.is_err()
        return fail(
            code: "unasked",
            msg: "HEROES_RUNTIME is set to `" + shell.env_written(name: "HEROES_RUNTIME") + "`, which is not UTF-8, so clang cannot be asked about the directory the compiler searches"
        )
    return hint

function message_of_words(got: [str]?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# What this machine says to one absence: ok with its own words where it lacks
# the thing, an error naming what refutes the compiler where it does not.
function asked(scratch: str, search: [str], one: Absence) -> str?
    shell.mkdir_all(scratch)?

    if one.kind == "pkg-config"
        tool = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--version"])?

        if tool.started
            return fail(code: "present", msg: "`pkg-config` runs on this machine")
        return ok("`pkg-config`, which `pkgconfig \"" + one.name + "\"` asks, does not start here")

    # A package, not installed or installed with its flags out of reach: the
    # flags must be refused here, and whether `pkg-config` reads the package
    # itself must agree with the headline (defect 292). It is asked in the two
    # spellings the compiler asks it in (`selfhost/cli/pkgconfig_answer.hero`'s
    # `installed`); a `.pc` that only its third question, the search path, finds
    # reads as not installed here, so a compiler saying *installed* of one is
    # judged, a red and never a skip.
    if one.kind == "package" || one.kind == "installed"
        answer = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--cflags", "--libs", "--", one.name])?

        if !answer.started
            return fail(
                code: "present",
                msg: "this machine has no `pkg-config` whose answer could say the package `" + one.name + "` is out of reach"
            )

        if answer.code == 0
            return fail(code: "present", msg: "`pkg-config` knows the package `" + one.name + "` on this machine")
        read = reads_alone(scratch: scratch, package: one.name)?

        if one.kind == "package" && read
            return fail(
                code: "present",
                msg: "`pkg-config` reads the package `" + one.name + "` on this machine, so it is installed"
            )

        if one.kind == "installed" && !read
            return fail(
                code: "present",
                msg: "`pkg-config` does not read the package `" + one.name + "` on this machine, so nothing says it is installed"
            )

        if one.kind == "installed"
            return ok("the package `" + one.name + "`, which `pkg-config` reads here and whose flags it cannot give")
        return ok("the package `" + one.name + "`, which `pkg-config` does not know here")
    probe = scratch + "/absence-probe.c"
    write_file(path: probe, text: "#include <" + one.name + ">\n")?
    words: [str] @= ["clang", "-E", "-x", "c"]

    for word in search
        words @ words.push(word)

    words @ words.push(probe)
    words @ words.push("-o")
    words @ words.push(scratch + "/absence-probe.i")
    clang = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: words)?

    if !clang.started
        return fail(
            code: "unasked",
            msg: "clang could not be started to ask whether `" + one.name + "` is on this machine"
        )

    if clang.code == 0
        return fail(code: "present", msg: "clang finds `" + one.name + "` on the include path the compiler searches")

    if !core_strings.contains(s: clang.err, substr: "'" + one.name + "' file not found")
        return fail(
            code: "unasked",
            msg: "clang refused `#include <" + one.name + ">` for another reason: " + first_line(clang.err)
        )
    return ok("`" + one.name + "`, which clang does not find on the include path the compiler searches")

# Whether `pkg-config` reads `package` itself, its requirements not followed:
# either of the two spellings the compiler asks answering (pkgconf takes
# `--maximum-traverse-depth=1`, pkg-config 0.29.2 refuses it and reads a plain
# `--modversion` alone, `pkgconfig_answer.installed`'s measurement).
function reads_alone(scratch: str, package: str) -> bool?
    for words in [["pkg-config", "--maximum-traverse-depth=1", "--modversion", "--", package], ["pkg-config", "--modversion", "--", package]]
        read = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: words)?

        if read.started && read.code == 0
            return ok(true)

    return ok(false)

# **A library a `link` group names, asked of the machine before a row that
# binds it runs** (defect 2481). The compiler tells a `link` library its
# linker did not find as `ffi_missing_library`, which no spelling above
# reads, so a case whose header is its own and whose library the machine
# lacks was judged and read red: panel 245's two rows over the platform's
# `libz` on the CI's Windows leg, which has no `z.lib`. A row that rests on
# a library of the platform's asks first, and here, never by the platform's
# name: clang links an empty program, and the same program with `-l<name>`,
# and only the second refused is the machine lacking the library. An empty
# program clang will not link, or a clang that does not start, asks nothing,
# so the row runs and is judged, the loud way.
constant LINK_WORD: str
    " link "

# Whether `source` holds an `extern` group whose `link` names `library`, the
# second witness a skip asks of (`binds` reads a group's header and package).
function links(source: str, library: str) -> bool
    for row in strings.lines(source)
        if !core_strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: "extern ")
            continue
        rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "extern ")
        header = quoted(rest)

        if header.is_err()
            continue
        tail = rest.slice(from: header.must().after, to: rest.len())

        if !core_strings.has_prefix(s: tail, prefix: LINK_WORD)
            continue
        named = quoted(tail.slice(from: LINK_WORD.len(), to: tail.len()))

        if !named.is_err() && named.must().value == library
            return true

    return false

# What this machine's linker says of `library`: ok with its words where clang
# links an empty program and refuses it with `-l<library>`; an error where it
# links both (`present`), or where it can say nothing (`unasked`).
function library_lacked(scratch: str, library: str) -> str?
    shell.mkdir_all(scratch)?
    program = scratch + "/library-probe.c"
    write_file(path: program, text: "int main(void) { return 0; }\n")?
    plain = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: ["clang", program, "-o", scratch + "/library-probe-plain" + shell.exe_suffix()])?

    if !plain.started
        return fail(
            code: "unasked",
            msg: "clang could not be started to ask whether this machine links library `" + library + "`"
        )

    if plain.code != 0
        return fail(
            code: "unasked",
            msg: "clang links no empty program here, so it cannot say whether library `" + library + "` is on this machine: " + first_line(plain.err)
        )
    linked = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: ["clang", program, "-l" + library, "-o", scratch + "/library-probe" + shell.exe_suffix()])?

    if linked.code == 0
        return fail(code: "present", msg: "clang links library `" + library + "` on this machine")
    return ok(
        "the library `" + library + "`, which clang's linker does not find here, where it links an empty program: " + first_line(linked.err)
    )

# How a message names an absence.
function shown(one: Absence) -> str
    if one.kind == "pkg-config"
        return "`pkg-config`"

    if one.kind == "installed"
        return "what the package `" + one.name + "` requires"
    return "the " + one.kind + " `" + one.name + "`"

# What a case would bind for an absence to be its own: a header, or a package.
function bound_as(one: Absence) -> str
    if one.kind == "header"
        return "header"
    return "package"

function message_of(got: str?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# The directory a case sits in, which clang searches as the compiler does: an
# `extern "mylib.h"` finds a header beside its `.hero` file.
function directory_of(path: str) -> str
    cut: i64 @= -1
    i: i64 @= 0

    while i < path.len()
        if path[i] == 47
            cut @ i
        i @ i + 1

    if cut < 0
        return "."
    return path.slice(from: 0, to: cut)

function first_line(text: str) -> str
    return strings.split_on(text: text, byte: 10)[0]

# Whether `row` opens the notes that carry `pkg-config`'s answer under a
# package's headline: its words, or that it gave none (defect 326).
function opens_quotation(row: str) -> bool
    return core_strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: NOTE + strings.trimmed(SAID)) || row == NOTE + NO_REASON

# Whether `row` is the way out that closes them, under either headline.
function closes_quotation(row: str) -> bool
    return row == NOTE + NOT_INSTALLED_WAY || row == NOTE + INSTALLED_WAY || row == NOTE + SILENT_WAY

# The expectation as the compiler would write it on THIS machine: each
# `pkg-config` answer an `ffi_pkgconfig` message quotes is replaced by what this
# machine's `pkg-config` answers for that package, and every line of the
# compiler's own around it is left as written.
#
# **The answer is the machine's, and so is its wording**, measured on panel
# 188's recorded runs (`scratchpad/188-ffi-pragmatist/q5/`, 2026-10-03):
# Debian's pkgconf 1.8.1 ends with `Package 'zz9', required by
# 'virtual:world', not found` where this Mac's 3.0.7 says `Package 'zz9' not
# found`, and nothing else differs. Three expectations quote it (defects 226 and
# 237, and `ffi-records-only-group-cannot-claim-its-package`), all written on
# this Mac, so the Linux legs skipped all three in silence until defect 246, and
# a judgement on the file's own words would turn them red there for a fact
# about pkgconf. What is judged instead is what the compiler promises: it quotes
# the answer of the machine it runs on, and every word of its own around the
# quotation. The quoted lines in the file are one machine's, kept for the
# reader.
#
# Under either package headline, not installed or installed with its flags out
# of reach (defect 292), the quotation runs from its opening note through the
# way out, which the installed headline words by whether `pkg-config` said
# anything, so the way is this machine's too.
#
# An expectation the frame does not fit, or a machine with no `pkg-config`, one
# that gives the package's flags, or one whose answer is not text, leaves the
# expectation as written: the case is then judged on the file's own bytes.
function quoted_here(scratch: str, expected: str) -> str
    rows = strings.split_on(text: expected, byte: 10)
    out: [str] @= []
    package: str @= ""
    kind: str @= ""
    changed: bool @= false
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < rows.len()
        row = rows[at]
        named = absence_in(row)

        if !named.is_err()
            package @ ""

            if named.must().kind == "package" || named.must().kind == "installed"
                package @ named.must().name
                kind @ named.must().kind

        if package != "" && opens_quotation(row)
            end = closing(rows: rows, from: at)

            if !end.is_err()
                notes = quotation(scratch: scratch, package: package, kind: kind)

                if !notes.is_err()
                    for note in notes.must()
                        out @ out.push(note)

                    changed @ true
                    package @ ""
                    at @ end.must() + 1
                    continue
        out @ out.push(row)
        at @ at + 1

    if !changed
        return expected
    return out.join("\n")

# The index of the way out that closes the quotation opening at `from`.
function closing(rows: [str], from: i64) -> i64?
    at: i64 @= from

    while at < rows.len()
        if closes_quotation(rows[at])
            return ok(at)
        at @ at + 1

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no closing note")

# This machine's answer for `package`, as the compiler's notes carry it under
# the headline `kind` names, asked with the compiler's own words
# (`selfhost/cli/libraries.hero`'s `resolve_pkgconfigs`).
function quotation(scratch: str, package: str, kind: str) -> [str]?
    ran = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--cflags", "--libs", "--", package])?

    if !ran.started || ran.code == 0
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "no refusal of this machine's to quote")
    return ok(quoted_notes(answer: ran.err, kind: kind))

# The notes the compiler writes under a package's headline for `pkg-config`'s
# complaint `answer`: its words (`notes_of`), then the way out, the installed
# headline's naming what pkg-config said it lacks only where it said anything.
function quoted_notes(answer: str, kind: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= notes_of(answer)
    way: str @= NOT_INSTALLED_WAY

    if kind == "installed"
        way @ INSTALLED_WAY

        if said_of(answer).len() == 0
            way @ SILENT_WAY
    out @ out.push(NOTE + way)
    return out

# `pkg-config`'s complaint as the compiler reads it before it words a note:
# `\r\n` read as one line end where the bytes enter (`pkgconfig_answer.complaint`,
# defect 285), then its ends trimmed of spaces, tabs and line ends (`worded`,
# `selfhost/cli/strings.hero`'s `trimmed`), a lone return kept. Until defect 328
# the trim came first here, so the last `\r\n` lost its `\n` and left a return
# the compiler no longer writes.
function said_of(answer: str) -> str
    return driver_trimmed(pairs_joined(answer))

# `pkg-config`'s complaint as the compiler's notes carry it: nothing said is
# the note that says so (defect 326); else `pkg-config said: ` and the
# complaint, each control character but a line end written by its code
# (`selfhost/cli/shell_split.hero`'s `shown`), then each line trimmed of spaces
# and tabs and kept where it holds anything (`selfhost/emit/ffi_lookup.hero`'s
# `lines` and `trimmed`, read by `ffi_build.pkgconfig_problem`).
function notes_of(answer: str) -> [str]
    said = said_of(answer)

    if said.len() == 0
        return [NOTE + NO_REASON]
    notes: [str] @= []

    for line in strings.split_on(text: SAID + written_controls(said), byte: 10)
        note = blanks_trimmed(line)

        if note.len() > 0
            notes @ notes.push(NOTE + note)

    return notes

# Spaces, tabs and line ends off both ends, and a return kept.
function driver_trimmed(text: str) -> str
    head: i64 @= 0

    while head < text.len() && (text[head] == 32 || text[head] == 9 || text[head] == 10)
        head @ head + 1

    tail: i64 @= text.len()

    while tail > head && (text[tail - 1] == 32 || text[tail - 1] == 9 || text[tail - 1] == 10)
        tail @ tail - 1

    return text.slice(from: head, to: tail)

# Spaces and tabs off both ends.
function blanks_trimmed(text: str) -> str
    head: i64 @= 0

    while head < text.len() && (text[head] == 32 || text[head] == 9)
        head @ head + 1

    tail: i64 @= text.len()

    while tail > head && (text[tail - 1] == 32 || text[tail - 1] == 9)
        tail @ tail - 1

    return text.slice(from: head, to: tail)

# `\r\n` as `\n`; a lone return is left where it is.
function pairs_joined(text: str) -> str
    parts: [str] @= []
    start: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at + 1 < text.len()
        if text[at] == 13 && text[at + 1] == 10
            parts @ parts.push(text.slice(from: start, to: at))
            start @ at + 1
        at @ at + 1

    parts @ parts.push(text.slice(from: start, to: text.len()))
    return parts.join("")

# `text` with every character the compiler does not write as itself written by
# its code, `<U+000D>`, as `shell_split.shown` writes it with its lines kept: a
# C0 control but the line end, DEL, and a C1 control (U+0080 to U+009F, whose
# UTF-8 is 0xC2 and 0x80 to 0x9F). Until defect 328 such an answer was refused
# here, and its case judged on the file's own bytes.
function written_controls(text: str) -> str
    pieces: [str] @= []
    start: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < text.len()
        byte = text[at]
        code: i64 @= -1
        width: i64 @= 1

        if byte < 32 && byte != 10 || byte == 127
            code @ to_i64(byte).must()

        if byte == 194 && at + 1 < text.len() && text[at + 1] >= 128 && text[at + 1] <= 159
            code @ to_i64(text[at + 1]).must()
            width @ 2

        if code >= 0
            pieces @ pieces.push(text.slice(from: start, to: at))
            pieces @ pieces.push("<U+" + hex4(code) + ">")
            start @ at + width
        at @ at + width

    pieces @ pieces.push(text.slice(from: start, to: text.len()))
    return pieces.join("")

# `code`, below 256, as four upper-case hexadecimal digits.
function hex4(code: i64) -> str
    digits = "0123456789ABCDEF"
    return "00" + digits.slice(from: code / 16, to: code / 16 + 1) + digits.slice(from: code % 16, to: code % 16 + 1)

# **What the suites' own tests plant**, here rather than in each suite so that
# the eight that skip for a missing library ask one stand-in the same thing.

# A header no machine has, so clang's answer about it is the same everywhere.
constant NOWHERE: str
    "heroes-a-header-no-machine-has.h"

# The compiler's words for a header clang did not find, as
# `selfhost/emit/ffi_build.hero` writes them, `tail` after the header's close
# and `at` the place it is told at.
function missing_header_said(header: str, tail: str, at: str) -> str
    return header_open() + header + HEADER_CLOSE + tail + "\n  at " + at + "\n"

# Where the compiler tells that a header is not on the include path,
# `<line>:<column>` in `source`: the first declaration of the first group
# naming `header`, as `selfhost/emit/ffi_lookup.hero`'s `group_head` answers
# (and for a header another includes, the group naming that other,
# `header_reach.hero`'s owner). A declaration is the first line under the
# group's head that is indented and holds code, so a comment above it, its
# documentation, is passed over. An error where no group of `source` names
# the header, or its group declares nothing. Until defect 449 (2026-10-07)
# every plant here stood at `:1:1`, the group's head, where the compiler's
# stands at its first declaration; the defect 328 test *what the compiler
# writes for a library a machine lacks* holds this to the compiler over five
# shapes.
function group_place(source: str, header: str) -> str?
    at: i64 @= 0
    in_group: bool @= false

    for row in strings.lines(source)
        at @ at + 1

        if core_strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: "extern ")
            named = quoted(strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "extern "))
            in_group @ !named.is_err() && named.must().value == header
            continue
        code = strings.trimmed(row)

        if !in_group || code.len() == 0 || code[0] == 35
            continue

        if row[0] != 32
            in_group @ false
            continue
        spaces: i64 @= 0

        while row[spaces] == 32
            spaces @ spaces + 1

        return ok(at.to_str() + ":" + (spaces + 1).to_str())

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no group of the source names `" + header + "` and declares something")

# What the compiler says of a header the group naming it in `source` cannot
# open, `source` written at `path`: `HEADER_LOOKED` after the header's close,
# told at `group_place`. What a test hands its stand-in, so a plant stands
# where the compiler's own would (defect 449).
function header_said(path: str, source: str, header: str) -> str?
    place = group_place(source: source, header: header)?
    return ok(missing_header_said(header: header, tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: path + ":" + place))

# The compiler's headline for a package `pkg-config` does not know, told at
# `at`, as `selfhost/cli/pkgconfig_answer.hero`'s `worded` and
# `selfhost/emit/ffi_build.hero`'s `pkgconfig_problem` write it, notes left out.
function package_said(package: str, at: str) -> str
    return package_open() + package + PACKAGE_CLOSE + "\n  at " + at + "\n"

# The compiler's words where `pkg-config` is not on this machine, told at `at`
# (`pkgconfig_answer.unasked`, defect 249): `ffi_pkgconfig` at the package's
# string, and the way out under it. They were a bare line with no code, no
# place and no note until that defect, and this net planted the bare line
# until defect 328.
function tool_said(package: str, at: str) -> str
    return package_open() + package + UNASKED_CLOSE + TOOL_OPEN + package + TOOL_CLOSE + "\n  at " + at + "\n" + NOTE + TOOL_WAY + "\n"

# A stand-in compiler: whatever its verb, it answers the first `.hero` it is
# handed with that case's `.said` file on stderr, at exit 1, so a test plants
# what the compiler says beside the case. POSIX, as every script case here.
function said_stub(scratch: str) -> str?
    shell.mkdir_all(scratch)?
    path = scratch + "/stub-said.sh"
    write_file(path: path, text: "#!/bin/sh\nfor word in \"$@\"; do\n    case \"$word\" in\n        *.hero) cat \"${word%.hero}.said\" 1>&2; exit 1 ;;\n    esac\ndone\nexit 2\n")?
    made = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", path])?

    if made.code != 0
        return fail(code: "cannot_run", msg: "cannot make " + path + " executable")
    return ok(path)

# A case planted for those tests: `<dir>/<name>.hero`, one group binding
# `bound`, and beside it what `said_stub` answers for it, that `missing` is not
# on the include path, told at the group's first declaration, where the
# compiler tells it (`group_place`). Answers the case's path.
function planted_case(dir: str, name: str, bound: str, missing: str) -> str?
    source = dir + "/" + name + ".hero"
    text = "extern \"" + bound + "\"\n    function seven() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven())\n"
    place = group_place(source: text, header: bound)?
    write_file(path: source, text: text)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/" + name + ".said", text: missing_header_said(header: missing, tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: source + ":" + place))?
    return ok(source)

## Tests

test "the four spellings name what is missing, where, and a header reached through another names its owner"
    direct = absences(missing_header_said(header: "gone.h", tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: "x.hero:2:5"))
    assert direct.len() == 1
    assert direct[0].kind == "header"
    assert direct[0].name == "gone.h"
    assert direct[0].owner == ""
    assert direct[0].code == "ffi_missing_header"
    assert direct[0].file == "x.hero"
    assert direct[0].line == 2
    deep = absences(
        "error[ffi_missing_header]: `sys/wait.h` is not on this machine's include path, and `outer.h`, the header this group names, includes it at line 7\n"
    )
    assert deep.len() == 1
    assert deep[0].name == "sys/wait.h"
    assert deep[0].owner == "outer.h"
    assert deep[0].line == 0

    # A package's name may hold a backtick, so it runs to the line's last
    # close; the brief form carries its place at the head of its line.
    ticked = absences("p.hero:9:26: error[ffi_pkgconfig]: the library `a`b` is not installed on this machine\n")
    assert ticked.len() == 1
    assert ticked[0].kind == "package"
    assert ticked[0].name == "a`b"
    assert ticked[0].code == "ffi_pkgconfig"
    assert ticked[0].file == "p.hero"
    assert ticked[0].line == 9

    # The sentence about `pkg-config`, at its package's string since defect
    # 249, keeps no code here (the record's comment says why) and its place.
    tool = absences(tool_said(package: "raylib", at: "x.hero:1:26"))
    assert tool.len() == 1
    assert tool[0].kind == "pkg-config"
    assert tool[0].name == "raylib"
    assert tool[0].code == ""
    assert tool[0].file == "x.hero"
    assert tool[0].line == 1

    # Defect 292's headline: a package installed, whose flags pkg-config
    # cannot give, a backtick in its name as well.
    installed = absences(
        "p.hero:9:26: error[ffi_pkgconfig]: the library `a`b` is installed, and pkg-config cannot give its flags\n"
    )
    assert installed.len() == 1
    assert installed[0].kind == "installed"
    assert installed[0].name == "a`b"
    assert installed[0].code == "ffi_pkgconfig"
    assert installed[0].line == 9

    # A line in no spelling of the compiler's names nothing: the old stub's
    # text, and a refusal clang gave on a header the machine has.
    assert absences("x.hero:1:1: error[ffi_pkgconfig]: pkg-config does not know raylib\n").len() == 0
    assert absences("error[ffi_return_type]: the header says `seven` returns `long`\n").len() == 0

test "an absence the expectation or a mark makes the case's subject is judged, however its words change"
    told = missing_header_said(header: "gone.h", tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: "c.hero:4:5")
    expected = Witnesses(
        named: absences(told),
        reported: ["4:ffi_missing_header"],
        marked: ["c.hero:4:ffi_missing_header"],
        path: "c.hero"
    )
    reworded = absences(
        "error[ffi_missing_header]: `gone.h` is not on this machine's include path — clang looked and found nothing\n  at c.hero:4:5\n"
    )
    assert pinned(one: reworded[0], seen: expected)

    # The same header blamed on another group, at another line, is still the
    # same library: the compiler's words changing about it.
    blamed = absences(
        "error[ffi_missing_header]: `gone.h` is not on this machine's include path, and `b.h`, the header this group names, includes it at line 1\n  at c.hero:9:5\n"
    )
    assert pinned(one: blamed[0], seen: expected)

    # Another name at the place the case pins: a compiler that misnames the
    # header it did not find (defect 232's class) is judged.
    misnamed = absences(
        missing_header_said(header: "gone", tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: "c.hero:4:5")
    )
    assert pinned(one: misnamed[0], seen: expected)

    # An expectation whose words are past reading still pins its place, and
    # so does a mark where the expectation lost the code.
    unread = Witnesses(named: [], reported: ["4:ffi_missing_header"], marked: [], path: "c.hero")
    assert pinned(one: reworded[0], seen: unread)
    marked = Witnesses(
        named: [],
        reported: ["4:ffi_missing_heder"],
        marked: ["c.hero:4:ffi_missing_header"],
        path: "c.hero"
    )
    assert pinned(one: reworded[0], seen: marked)

    # Another library at another place is a candidate for the machine's answer,
    # and so is one told in another file, whose lines are not the case's.
    other = absences(
        missing_header_said(header: "sys/wait.h", tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: "c.hero:12:5")
    )
    assert !pinned(one: other[0], seen: expected)
    elsewhere = absences(
        missing_header_said(header: "sys/wait.h", tail: HEADER_LOOKED, at: "m.hero:4:5")
    )
    assert !pinned(one: elsewhere[0], seen: expected)

    # Unless that file's own mark places it: a module's mark is its own.
    in_module = Witnesses(named: [], reported: [], marked: ["m.hero:4:ffi_missing_header"], path: "c.hero")
    assert pinned(one: elsewhere[0], seen: in_module)

    # And a `pkg-config` that does not start is not the package the
    # expectation says is not installed: the Windows box's answer, told at the
    # same string under the same code, and still not pinned there.
    package = package_said(package: "p", at: "c.hero:1:26")
    asking = Witnesses(
        named: absences(package),
        reported: ["1:ffi_pkgconfig"],
        marked: ["c.hero:1:ffi_pkgconfig"],
        path: "c.hero"
    )
    tool = absences(tool_said(package: "p", at: "c.hero:1:26"))
    assert tool[0].line == 1
    assert !pinned(one: tool[0], seen: asking)

    # A package said to be installed where the case pins it as not: the same
    # code at the same place, so the case tests that message and is judged.
    installed = absences(
        "error[ffi_pkgconfig]: the library `p` is installed, and pkg-config cannot give its flags\n  at c.hero:1:26\n"
    )
    assert pinned(one: installed[0], seen: asking)

test "a case binds its groups' headers and packages, read as the language writes a string"
    bound = binds(
        "extern \"stdio.h\" pkgconfig \"zz\"  #~ ffi_pkgconfig\n    function f()\n\nextern \"a\\\"b.h\" link \"m\"\n    function g()\n# extern \"prose.h\" in a comment binds nothing\n"
    )
    assert bound.headers == ["stdio.h", "a\"b.h"]
    assert bound.packages == ["zz"]
    assert needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "header", name: "stdio.h", owner: ""))
    assert needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "header", name: "deep.h", owner: "stdio.h"))
    assert !needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "header", name: "deep.h", owner: ""))
    assert !needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "header", name: "prose.h", owner: ""))
    assert needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "package", name: "zz", owner: ""))
    assert needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "pkg-config", name: "zz", owner: ""))
    assert needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "installed", name: "zz", owner: ""))
    assert !needs(bound: bound, one: an(kind: "package", name: "other", owner: ""))

test "clang is asked, and a header the machine has refutes the compiler"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    search = ["-I", "."]
    has = asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: an(kind: "header", name: "stdio.h", owner: ""))
    assert has.is_err()
    assert core_strings.contains(s: message_of(has), substr: "clang finds `stdio.h`")
    lacks = asked(
        scratch: scratch,
        search: search,
        one: an(kind: "header", name: "heroes-absence-a-header-no-machine-has.h", owner: "")
    )
    assert !lacks.is_err()
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "pkg-config is asked as the compiler asks it, and a pkg-config that runs refutes its absence"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    search: [str] = []
    nobody = an(kind: "package", name: "heroes-absence-a-package-no-machine-has", owner: "")
    tool = an(kind: "pkg-config", name: "heroes-absence-a-package-no-machine-has", owner: "")
    here = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--version"])
    assert !here.is_err()

    # Each branch asserts the machine's own fact: where `pkg-config` runs, it
    # does not know the package and it is not missing; where it does not, the
    # package cannot be said to be uninstalled by it, and it is missing.
    if here.must().started
        assert !asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: nobody).is_err()
        assert asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: tool).is_err()
    else
        assert asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: nobody).is_err()
        assert !asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: tool).is_err()
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 328's witness for defect 292's headline: a package installed whose
# flags `pkg-config` cannot give is the machine's answer only where it reads the
# package and refuses its flags. A `.pc` named by its path is read as that file
# by every `pkg-config` measured (`selfhost/cli/libraries.hero`'s test of defect
# 292), so one requiring a package no machine has is installed and out of
# reach here; a package no machine has is neither, so a compiler calling it
# installed is refuted, and one calling the `.pc` not installed is too.
test "fixedbugs: a package said to be installed with its flags out of reach is asked both halves (defect 328)"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    here = shell.attempted(scratch: scratch, program: "pkg-config", words: ["pkg-config", "--version"])
    assert !here.is_err()
    search: [str] = []
    pc = scratch + "/broken328.pc"
    assert !write_file(path: pc, text: "Name: broken328\nDescription: requires what no machine has\nVersion: 1.0\nRequires: heroes-absence-a-package-no-machine-has\nCflags: -I/nowhere\n").is_err()
    broken = an(kind: "installed", name: pc, owner: "")
    nobody = an(kind: "installed", name: "heroes-absence-a-package-no-machine-has", owner: "")
    called_absent = an(kind: "package", name: pc, owner: "")

    if here.must().started
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: broken).must(),
            substr: "which `pkg-config` reads here and whose flags it cannot give"
        )
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: message_of(asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: nobody)),
            substr: "so nothing says it is installed"
        )
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: message_of(asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: called_absent)),
            substr: "so it is installed"
        )
    else
        assert asked(scratch: scratch, search: search, one: broken).is_err()
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 328: every spelling the net reads the compiler's words by is a piece
# one literal of the compiler's source holds, and this reads each out of the
# file that writes it, so a sentence reworded there is a red here naming the
# piece and the file, where until that day a lagging copy read on in silence.
test "fixedbugs: the spellings are the compiler's own, read out of the files that write them (defect 328)"
    parted: [str] @= []

    for one in spellings()
        source = read_file(one.file)
        assert !source.is_err()

        if !core_strings.contains(s: source.must(), substr: one.heard)
            parted @ parted.push(one.file + " no longer holds " + one.heard)

    assert parted.join("\n") == ""
    assert spellings().len() == 17

# DEFECT 450: the pieces above are spelled in this module and in no other of
# the net, so no copy can lag the compiler where the test above does not look.
# Every module of `tests/harness/` but this one is read, its comment lines left
# out, since a piece quoted in prose explains where one in code is a copy. The
# pieces any sentence may hold (`NOTE`, `PACKAGE_HEAD`, `OWNER_OPEN`,
# `OWNER_TAIL`, the two codes) are not asked; each stands in the compiler's
# words beside one that is. Until that day three suites spelled
# `HEADER_LOOKED` six times and `suite_golden` a group's owner, a package's
# close and pkg-config's note, matching the compiler only while it said so.
test "fixedbugs: the compiler's words for a missing library are spelled in this module alone (defect 450)"
    pieces = [
        HEADER_CLOSE
        HEADER_LOOKED
        OWNER_CLOSE
        PACKAGE_MARK
        PACKAGE_CLOSE
        INSTALLED_CLOSE
        UNASKED_CLOSE
        TOOL_OPEN
        TOOL_CLOSE
        SAID
        NO_REASON
        NOT_INSTALLED_WAY
        INSTALLED_WAY
        SILENT_WAY
        TOOL_WAY
    ]
    copies: [str] @= []
    read: i64 @= 0

    for path in shell.list_entries(scratch: "build/harness-selftest", dir: "tests/harness").must()
        if !core_strings.has_suffix(s: path, suffix: ".hero") || core_strings.has_suffix(s: path, suffix: "/absence.hero")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)
        assert !text.is_err()
        read @ read + 1
        at: i64 @= 0

        for row in strings.lines(text.must())
            at @ at + 1

            if core_strings.has_prefix(s: strings.trimmed(row), prefix: "#")
                continue

            for piece in pieces
                if core_strings.contains(s: row, substr: as_written(piece))
                    copies @ copies.push(path + ":" + at.to_str() + ": " + piece)

    assert copies.join("\n") == ""

    # Forty-one modules beside this one on the day: a walk reaching fewer is
    # not reading the net.
    assert read >= 41

# DEFECT 328 asked of the compiler itself: what it writes for a library a
# machine lacks, over cases of every shape, is what this module reads, and the
# notes it writes under a package's headline are the ones `quoted_notes`
# rebuilds from the same answer of `pkg-config`. That answer comes from a
# stand-in on the compiler's PATH, so each shape is asked on every POSIX
# machine: a Mac's lines, `\r\n` ones (defect 285), nothing and blanks (326),
# and a tab and lone returns, which the compiler writes by their codes; and
# under the headline of a package installed with its flags out of reach (292).
# A `pkg-config` not on this machine is a PATH holding clang alone. Until that
# day the CRLF answer was refused by the rebuild, the silent one rebuilt as
# `pkg-config said:`, and the installed headline named nothing at all.
test "fixedbugs: what the compiler writes for a library a machine lacks is read as it writes it (defect 328)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/said328"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    calling = "    function seven() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven())\n"

    # A header no machine has, named by the group, told at the group's first
    # declaration, and the same reached through a header the group names.
    direct = dir + "/direct.hero"
    told = absences(
        built_said(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: direct, source: "extern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n" + calling, bin: "", alone: false).must()
    )
    assert told.len() == 1
    assert told[0].kind == "header"
    assert told[0].name == NOWHERE
    assert told[0].owner == ""
    assert told[0].code == HEADER_CODE
    assert told[0].file == direct
    assert told[0].line == 2
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/outer328.h", text: "#include \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\nint seven(void);\n").is_err()
    through = absences(
        built_said(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: dir + "/through.hero", source: "extern \"outer328.h\"\n" + calling, bin: "", alone: false).must()
    )
    assert through.len() == 1
    assert through[0].name == NOWHERE
    assert through[0].owner == "outer328.h"

    # DEFECT 449: a plant stands where the compiler tells it, its headline
    # and its `at` line byte for byte, for a group on the first line, one
    # after a comment and a blank line whose member is documented, a second
    # group after one the machine has, a constant, and a record.
    shapes = [
        "extern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n" + calling
        "# a program\n\nextern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n    # what seven does\n    function seven() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven())\n"
        "extern \"stdio.h\"\n    function puts(s: cstr) -> i32\n\nextern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n" + calling
        "extern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n    constant SEVEN: i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(SEVEN)\n"
        "extern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n    record Seven\n        n: i32\n    function seven() -> Seven\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven().n)\n"
    ]
    places: [str] @= []
    count: i64 @= 0

    for text in shapes
        path = dir + "/placed-" + count.to_str() + ".hero"
        planted = header_said(path: path, source: text, header: NOWHERE).must()
        built = built_said(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: path, source: text, bin: "", alone: false).must()
        assert core_strings.has_prefix(s: built, prefix: planted)
        places @ places.push(group_place(source: text, header: NOWHERE).must())
        count @ count + 1

    assert places == ["2:5", "5:5", "5:5", "2:5", "2:5"]

    # And what `planted_case` writes beside its case, which seven suites'
    # tests hand their stand-in, is what the compiler writes of that case.
    laid = planted_case(dir: dir, name: "planted449", bound: NOWHERE, missing: NOWHERE)
    assert !laid.is_err()
    case_said = read_file(dir + "/planted449.said")
    assert !case_said.is_err()
    case_source = read_file(laid.must())
    assert !case_source.is_err()
    case_built = built_said(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: compiler,
        path: laid.must(),
        source: case_source.must(),
        bin: "",
        alone: false
    )
    assert core_strings.has_prefix(s: case_built.must(), prefix: case_said.must())

    # A package, as a stand-in `pkg-config` answers for it.
    package = "heroes-absence-328-no-machine-has"
    asking = "extern \"stdio.h\" pkgconfig \"" + package + "\"\n" + calling
    answers = [
        "Package " + package + " was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPackage '" + package + "' not found\n"
        "Package " + package + " was not found in the pkg-config search path.\r\nPackage '" + package + "' not found\r\n"
        ""
        "  \r\n"
        "a\tb\rc\r\n"
    ]
    shape: i64 @= 0

    for answer in answers
        for installed in [false, true]
            bin = dir + "/bin-" + shape.to_str()
            assert !stand_in(dir: bin, answer: answer, installed: installed).is_err()
            err = built_said(
                scratch: scratch,
                compiler: compiler,
                path: dir + "/package-" + shape.to_str() + ".hero",
                source: asking,
                bin: bin,
                alone: false
            ).must()
            named = absences(err)
            kind: str @= "package"

            if installed
                kind @ "installed"
            assert named.len() == 1
            assert named[0].kind == kind
            assert named[0].name == package
            assert notes_in(err) == quoted_notes(answer: answer, kind: kind)
            shape @ shape + 1

    # No `pkg-config` at all: the compiler's PATH holds clang and nothing else.
    clang = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "/bin/sh", words: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "command -v clang"]).must()
    assert clang.code == 0
    only_clang = dir + "/clang-alone"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(only_clang).is_err()
    linked = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: "ln",
        words: ["ln", "-s", strings.trimmed(clang.out), only_clang + "/clang"]
    ).must()
    assert linked.code == 0
    unasked = built_said(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: compiler,
        path: dir + "/tool.hero",
        source: asking,
        bin: only_clang,
        alone: true
    ).must()
    tool = absences(unasked)
    assert tool.len() == 1
    assert tool[0].kind == "pkg-config"
    assert tool[0].name == package
    assert tool[0].line == 1
    assert notes_in(unasked) == [NOTE + TOOL_WAY]
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# PANEL 198, asked of the compiler itself: a package that ANSWERS with the words
# the list holds builds and runs on every POSIX machine, whichever linker it has.
# The stand-in on the compiler's PATH answers with `-isystem <dir>` and
# `-isystem<dir>` (the headers the program binds reach through them alone),
# `-pthread` and the two linker words the compiler's own link line already says;
# Apple's `ld` stops at those two words (*ld: unknown options*), so a build that
# passes there is one where they never reached a linker. The words beside them
# stay refused, and the refusal names the whole word: the one that moves a tag,
# a comma that carries a second option, a position-dependent word, a warning flag.
test "a package answering words the list holds builds and runs, and the words beside them stay refused (panel 198)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/said198"
    inc = dir + "/inc"
    sys = dir + "/sys"
    joined = dir + "/joined"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(inc).is_err()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(sys).is_err()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(joined).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: inc + "/pkgcase198.h", text: "#include \"pkgcase198_sys.h\"\n#include \"pkgcase198_joined.h\"\nstatic inline int seven198(void) { return SEVEN198 + NOUGHT198; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: sys + "/pkgcase198_sys.h", text: "#define SEVEN198 7\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: joined + "/pkgcase198_joined.h", text: "#define NOUGHT198 0\n").is_err()
    asking = "extern \"pkgcase198.h\" pkgconfig \"pkgcase198\"\n    function seven198() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven198())\n"
    bin = dir + "/bin-ok"
    admitted = "-I" + inc + " -isystem " + sys + " -isystem" + joined + " -pthread -Wl,--enable-new-dtags -Wl,--export-dynamic\n"
    assert !answering(dir: bin, answer: admitted).is_err()
    path = dir + "/ok.hero"
    err = built_said(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: path, source: asking, bin: bin, alone: false).must()
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: err, substr: "does not pass on")
    ran = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: path + ".out", words: [path + ".out"]).must()
    assert ran.code == 0
    assert ran.out == "7\n"

    for word in ["-Wl,--disable-new-dtags", "-Wl,--enable-new-dtags,--plugin,x.so", "-Wl,--whole-archive", "-Wno-error"]
        stood = dir + "/bin-" + word.len().to_str()
        assert !answering(dir: stood, answer: "-I" + inc + " " + word + "\n").is_err()
        said = built_said(
            scratch: scratch,
            compiler: compiler,
            path: dir + "/refused-" + word.len().to_str() + ".hero",
            source: asking,
            bin: stood,
            alone: false
        ).must()
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: said,
            substr: "answered with `" + word + "`, which this compiler does not pass on"
        )

    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# PANEL 198's premise, asked where it can be: `-Wl,--enable-new-dtags` is left out
# of a package's answer because it restates the tag a linker writes beside the
# admitted `-Wl,-rpath`, RUNPATH, on every ELF toolchain measured (GNU ld 2.42 and
# 2.44, LLD 22.1.8, aarch64 and x86-64). That is a claim about the world, so the
# day a toolchain writes the other tag (`--disable-new-dtags` is the word that
# moves it, and upstream GNU ld's own default) this fires, naming what depends on
# it: `selfhost/cli/pkgconfig_words.hero`'s `restates`. Where no `readelf` reads
# the binary, as on macOS, there is nothing to ask and the case returns.
test "an admitted -Wl,-rpath leaves a RUNPATH where readelf reads the binary, the tag the left-out word restates (panel 198)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/tag198"
    inc = dir + "/inc"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(inc).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: inc + "/pkgtag198.h", text: "static inline int seven198(void) { return 7; }\n").is_err()
    asking = "extern \"pkgtag198.h\" pkgconfig \"pkgtag198\"\n    function seven198() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven198())\n"
    bin = dir + "/bin"
    assert !answering(dir: bin, answer: "-I" + inc + " -Wl,-rpath,/opt/heroes-198-rpath\n").is_err()
    path = dir + "/tag.hero"
    err = built_said(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: path, source: asking, bin: bin, alone: false).must()
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: err, substr: "does not pass on")
    read = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "readelf", words: ["readelf", "-d", path + ".out"])

    if read.is_err()
        return
    dynamic = read.must()

    if dynamic.code != 0
        return
    assert core_strings.contains(s: dynamic.out, substr: "/opt/heroes-198-rpath")
    assert core_strings.contains(s: dynamic.out, substr: "(RUNPATH)")
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: dynamic.out, substr: "(RPATH)")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# PANEL 198's route V (defect 527), asked of the compiler itself: a value the list
# admits by its word is still judged as a value. A response file in a value's
# place (`@<file>`), which clang and the linkers read as more words, and GNU ld's
# `-l:<file>`, which names a file, are refused at the package's string, the
# message naming the words and why, and no binary is written. Until route V the
# compiler passed all of them on: panel 198's completeness critic had clang and
# the linker read options out of such a file through `-framework`, `-Wl,-rpath`
# and `-Wl,-framework`, and `libuv-static`'s `-l:libuv.a` ended at exit 2.
test "a package whose admitted word carries @<file> or -l:<file> is refused, naming it (panel 198, route V)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/said198v"
    inc = dir + "/inc"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(inc).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: inc + "/pkgv198.h", text: "static inline int seven198(void) { return 7; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/rsp.txt", text: "/opt/heroes-198-v\n").is_err()
    asking = "extern \"pkgv198.h\" pkgconfig \"pkgv198\"\n    function seven198() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven198())\n"
    shapes = [
        "-Wl,-rpath,@" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-Wl,-rpath -Wl,@" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-Wl,-framework,@" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-framework @" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-isystem @" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-L@" + dir + "/rsp.txt"
        "-l:libheroes198.a"
    ]
    shape: i64 @= 0

    for word in shapes
        why: str @= "a value beginning with `@` names a file"

        if core_strings.has_prefix(s: word, prefix: "-l:")
            why @ "`-l:<file>` names a file"
        stood = dir + "/bin-" + shape.to_str()
        assert !answering(dir: stood, answer: "-I" + inc + " " + word + "\n").is_err()
        path = dir + "/v-" + shape.to_str() + ".hero"
        said = built_said(
            scratch: scratch,
            compiler: compiler,
            path: path,
            source: asking,
            bin: stood,
            alone: false
        ).must()
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: said,
            substr: "answered with `" + word + "`, which this compiler does not pass on: " + why
        )
        assert !shell.exists(path + ".out")
        shape @ shape + 1

    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 528, asked of the compiler itself: a package whose answer names a
# library this machine does not have is told at the package's string,
# `ffi_missing_library` as defect 224 tells a `link` group's, naming the
# package and the word, where the build ended at exit 2 with the linker's text
# (`pkgconfig "libiodbc"` on this Mac). The answer is a `--libs` alone, one
# beside a `-L` that holds no such library, and the split `-l <name>` the list
# joins.
test "a package whose -l this machine has no library for is told at its string (defect 528)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/lacked528"
    empty = dir + "/lib"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(empty).is_err()
    asking = "extern \"stdlib.h\" pkgconfig \"pkg528\"\n    function abs(n: i32) -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(abs(-7))\n"
    shapes = [
        "-lheroes528absent"
        "-L" + empty + " -lheroes528absent"
        "-l heroes528absent"
    ]
    shape: i64 @= 0

    for answer in shapes
        stood = dir + "/bin-" + shape.to_str()
        assert !answering(dir: stood, answer: answer + "\n").is_err()
        path = dir + "/l-" + shape.to_str() + ".hero"
        said = built_said(
            scratch: scratch,
            compiler: compiler,
            path: path,
            source: asking,
            bin: stood,
            alone: false
        ).must()
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: said,
            substr: "error[ffi_missing_library]: this machine has no library called `heroes528absent` — `pkg-config` answered for `pkg528` with `-lheroes528absent`, and the linker looked\n  at " + path + ":1:29\n"
        )
        assert !core_strings.contains(s: said, substr: "internal error")
        assert !shell.exists(path + ".out")
        shape @ shape + 1

    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# PANEL 239's R1, asked of the compiler itself (defects 2363 and 2378): a C
# name a group binds that a library the program names for another group
# defines too, or a C library's name such a library defines again, is
# refused `ffi_defined_elsewhere` at exit 1 on the binding in every order of
# the libraries, where the program was built at exit 0 bound by the order: a
# wrong value, or a memory fault where the other definition takes another
# signature (Bus error on this Mac, SIGSEGV on Linux arm64, 2026-11-09).
# Since panel 238's walk (its R2) the libraries stand in the order of their
# modules' names, so a program is asked as written, with its `use` lines the
# other way round (one text, byte for byte), and with its group's module
# renamed to sort first, which turns the libraries round (one text but that
# name). A library is built with clang alone where it is shared and with the
# machine's `ar` where it is a file of objects; on Windows, which runs no
# script and has no `ar`, a library is a DLL clang builds, its names exported,
# and the shared case alone is asked (defect 2376).

# A file a case writes: its name in the case's folder and its text.
record Written
    name: str
    text: str

constant PICKER: str
    "extern \"a.h\" link \"a\"\n    function pick() -> i32\n    function a_only() -> i32\n\nfunction chosen() -> i32\n    return pick()\n\nfunction only() -> i32\n    return a_only()\n"

# Every file of `files` written into `dir`.
function wrote(dir: str, files: [Written]) -> ()?
    for one in files
        write_file(path: dir + "/" + one.name, text: one.text)?

    return ok()

# A module binding `bound` from `header` in `library`'s group, and answering
# it from `only`.
function other_module(header: str, library: str, bound: str) -> str
    return "extern \"" + header + "\" link \"" + library + "\"\n    function " + bound + "() -> i32\n\nfunction only() -> i32\n    return " + bound + "()\n"

# A program using `first` and `second`, printing `group`'s pick and `other`'s
# own answer.
function both_used(first: str, second: str, group: str, other: str) -> str
    return "use " + first + "\nuse " + second + "\n\nfunction main()\n    print(" + group + ".chosen())\n    print(" + other + ".only())\n"

# Whether this machine is Darwin, whose shared library is a `.dylib`.
function darwin(scratch: str) -> bool
    said = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "uname", words: ["uname", "-s"])
    return !said.is_err() && said.must().out == "Darwin\n"

# `lib<library>` in `dir` of `sources`, each a `.c` compiled: a file of
# objects by `ar`, or shared by clang alone where `shared`; on Windows
# `<library>.dll` and the import library `<library>.lib` beside it.
function c_library(scratch: str, dir: str, library: str, sources: [Written], shared: bool) -> ()?
    wrote(dir: dir, files: sources)?

    if shared && shell.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        dll: [str] @= ["clang", "-shared", "-o", dir + "/" + library + ".dll"]

        for one in sources
            dll @ dll.push(dir + "/" + one.name)

        made = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: dll)?

        if made.code != 0
            return fail(code: "not_built", msg: library + ".dll: " + made.out + made.err)
        return ok()
    words: [str] @= ["ar", "rcs", dir + "/lib" + library + ".a"]

    if shared && darwin(scratch)
        words @ [
            "clang"
            "-dynamiclib"
            "-install_name"
            "@rpath/lib" + library + ".dylib"
            "-o"
            dir + "/lib" + library + ".dylib"
        ]
    else if shared
        words @ ["clang", "-shared", "-o", dir + "/lib" + library + ".so"]

    for one in sources
        object = dir + "/" + one.name + ".o"
        made = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "clang", words: ["clang", "-c", "-fPIC", "-O0", dir + "/" + one.name, "-o", object])?

        if made.code != 0
            return fail(code: "not_built", msg: one.name + ": " + made.err)
        words @ words.push(object)

    made = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: words[0], words: words)?

    if made.code != 0
        return fail(code: "not_built", msg: "lib" + library + ": " + made.err)
    return ok()

# What the compiler says building `<dir>/<program>.hero` against the
# libraries in `dir`.
function built_against(scratch: str, compiler: str, dir: str, program: str) -> shell.Run
    return shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "build", dir + "/" + program + ".hero", "--library", dir, "-o", dir + "/" + program + ".out"]
    ).must()

# A refusal `ffi_defined_elsewhere` saying `headline` on `module`'s first
# binding, nothing written.
function refused_at(got: shell.Run, dir: str, module: str, headline: str, program: str) -> bool
    at = "error[ffi_defined_elsewhere]: " + headline
    return got.code == 1 && core_strings.contains(s: got.err, substr: at) && core_strings.contains(s: got.err, substr: "\n  at " + dir + "/" + module + ".hero:2:5\n") && !core_strings.contains(s: got.err, substr: "internal error") && !shell.exists(dir + "/" + program + ".out")

# A C file's text with its names exported where it is a DLL's, `X` before
# each, and nothing elsewhere.
function exported(text: str) -> str
    return "#ifdef _WIN32\n#define X __declspec(dllexport)\n#else\n#define X\n#endif\n" + text

# How many definitions a refusal names, a note each (defect 2384).
function definitions_named(said: str) -> i64
    return strings.split_text(text: said, separator: "  note: a definition is in `").len() - 1

# `said` with every `from` written `to`.
function renamed(said: str, from: str, to: str) -> str
    return strings.split_text(text: said, separator: from).join(to)

test "a C name two shared libraries define, or one the C library defines, is refused in every order (defects 2363 and 2376)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts() && shell.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/shared2363"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "a", sources: [Written(name: "pick_a.c", text: exported("X int pick(void) { return 1; }\n")), Written(name: "a_only.c", text: exported("X int a_only(void) { return 10; }\n"))], shared: true).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "b", sources: [Written(name: "pick_b.c", text: exported("X int pick(void) { return 2; }\n")), Written(name: "b_only.c", text: exported("X int b_only(void) { return 20; }\n"))], shared: true).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "q", sources: [Written(name: "q_only.c", text: exported("X int q_only(void) { return 30; }\n")), Written(name: "puts_q.c", text: exported("X int puts(int *out) { *out = 5; return 1; }\n"))], shared: true).is_err()
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "a.h", text: "int pick(void);\nint a_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "b.h", text: "int b_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "q.h", text: "int q_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "picker.hero", text: PICKER), Written(name: "apick.hero", text: PICKER), Written(name: "extra.hero", text: other_module(header: "b.h", library: "b", bound: "b_only")), Written(name: "quiet.hero", text: other_module(header: "q.h", library: "q", bound: "q_only")), Written(name: "printer.hero", text: "extern \"stdio.h\"\n    function puts(s: cstr lent) -> i32\n\nfunction chosen() -> i32\n    return puts(s: \"hello\".cstr())\n"), Written(name: "written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker", second: "extra", group: "picker", other: "extra")), Written(name: "sorted.hero", text: both_used(first: "extra", second: "picker", group: "picker", other: "extra")), Written(name: "flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "extra", group: "apick", other: "extra")), Written(name: "interpose.hero", text: both_used(first: "printer", second: "quiet", group: "printer", other: "quiet"))]).is_err()
    headline = "`pick`, bound here from library `a`, is also defined by library `b`, which module `extra` links, and a program holds one `pick`: which of the two its calls reach would rest on the order of the libraries, which no line of the program writes"
    written = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "written")
    assert refused_at(written, dir: dir, module: "picker", headline: headline, program: "written")

    # One note for each definition, named by the folder the program's words
    # gave (defect 2384); a Windows map names a library by its name and the
    # DLL it stands for, which is named in that folder.
    assert definitions_named(written.err) == 2

    if shell.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        assert core_strings.contains(s: written.err, substr: "  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/a.lib(a.dll)`\n")
        assert core_strings.contains(s: written.err, substr: "  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/b.lib(b.dll)`\n")
    else
        assert core_strings.contains(s: written.err, substr: "  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/liba.")
        assert core_strings.contains(s: written.err, substr: "  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/libb.")
    sorted = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "sorted")
    assert sorted.err == written.err
    flip = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "flip")
    assert refused_at(flip, dir: dir, module: "apick", headline: headline, program: "flip")
    assert renamed(said: flip.err, from: "apick", to: "picker") == written.err
    interposed = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "interpose")
    assert refused_at(
        interposed,
        dir: dir,
        module: "printer",
        headline: "`puts`, bound here from the C library, would be called from library `q`, which module `quiet` links, not from the C library",
        program: "interpose"
    )
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a C name two libraries of objects define is refused in every order, a definition no order loads among them, each named once (defects 2363 and 2384)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/archives2363"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    pick_a = Written(name: "pick_a.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 1; }\n")
    a_only = Written(name: "a_only.c", text: "int a_only(void) { return 10; }\n")
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "a", sources: [pick_a, a_only], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "w", sources: [Written(name: "pick_w.c", text: "int pick(int *out) { *out = 99; return 2; }\n"), Written(name: "w_only.c", text: "int w_only(void) { return 30; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "b2", sources: [Written(name: "b2.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 2; }\nint b_only(void) { return 20; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "bw", sources: [Written(name: "weak_b.c", text: "__attribute__((weak)) int pick(void) { return 3; }\nint b_only(void) { return 20; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "ab", sources: [Written(name: "ab.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 1; }\nint a_only(void) { return 10; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "bs", sources: [Written(name: "pick_b.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 2; }\n"), Written(name: "b_self.c", text: "int pick(void);\nint b_only(void) { return 10 * pick(); }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    picker2 = "extern \"a.h\" link \"ab\"\n    function pick() -> i32\n    function a_only() -> i32\n\nfunction chosen() -> i32\n    return pick()\n"
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "a.h", text: "int pick(void);\nint a_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "w.h", text: "int w_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "b.h", text: "int b_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "picker.hero", text: PICKER), Written(name: "apick.hero", text: PICKER), Written(name: "picker2.hero", text: picker2), Written(name: "apick2.hero", text: picker2), Written(name: "aux.hero", text: other_module(header: "w.h", library: "w", bound: "w_only")), Written(name: "extra2.hero", text: other_module(header: "b.h", library: "b2", bound: "b_only")), Written(name: "extraw.hero", text: other_module(header: "b.h", library: "bw", bound: "b_only")), Written(name: "extra_s.hero", text: other_module(header: "b.h", library: "bs", bound: "b_only")), Written(name: "sig_written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker", second: "aux", group: "picker", other: "aux")), Written(name: "sig_sorted.hero", text: both_used(first: "aux", second: "picker", group: "picker", other: "aux")), Written(name: "sig_flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "aux", group: "apick", other: "aux")), Written(name: "one_written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker", second: "extra2", group: "picker", other: "extra2")), Written(name: "one_flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "extra2", group: "apick", other: "extra2")), Written(name: "weak_written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker", second: "extraw", group: "picker", other: "extraw")), Written(name: "weak_flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "extraw", group: "apick", other: "extraw")), Written(name: "ab_written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker2", second: "extra_s", group: "picker2", other: "extra_s")), Written(name: "ab_flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick2", second: "extra_s", group: "apick2", other: "extra_s")), Written(name: "self_written.hero", text: both_used(first: "picker", second: "extra_s", group: "picker", other: "extra_s")), Written(name: "self_flip.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "extra_s", group: "apick", other: "extra_s"))]).is_err()
    whole = ", and a program holds one `pick`: this call, or that library's own calls to it, would reach the other library's"

    # `sig`, the defect's own: `libw`'s `pick` takes a pointer, and the order
    # that reached it died by a memory fault.
    sig = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "sig_written")
    assert refused_at(
        sig,
        dir: dir,
        module: "picker",
        headline: "`pick`, bound here from library `a`, is also defined by library `w`, which module `aux` links" + whole,
        program: "sig_written"
    )

    # Apple's linker names a duplicate's file by its absolute path and its
    # map by the folder the line gave, and each definition was told twice,
    # four notes for two (defect 2384, panel 239's blind seat): one note for
    # each, by the folder as the program's words wrote it, relative here.
    assert definitions_named(sig.err) == 2
    assert core_strings.contains(
        s: sig.err,
        substr: "  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/liba.a(pick_a.c.o)`\n  note: a definition is in `" + dir + "/libw.a(pick_w.c.o)`\n"
    )
    assert built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "sig_sorted").err == sig.err
    assert renamed(said: built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "sig_flip").err, from: "apick", to: "picker") == sig.err

    # `one`: the other library's file loaded for its own name holds the
    # duplicate, which Apple's linker takes in both orders and GNU ld refuses
    # in one; `weak`: a weak definition there, which Apple's linker takes in
    # both orders and GNU ld in one, so the headline says which.
    shapes = [
        ["one", "picker", "a", "b2", "extra2"]
        ["weak", "picker", "a", "bw", "extraw"]
        ["self", "picker", "a", "bs", "extra_s"]
        ["ab", "picker2", "ab", "bs", "extra_s"]
    ]

    for shape in shapes
        one = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: shape[0] + "_written")
        opening = "`pick`, bound here from library `" + shape[2] + "`, "
        assert refused_at(one, dir: dir, module: shape[1], headline: opening, program: shape[0] + "_written")
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: one.err,
            substr: "library `" + shape[3] + "`, which module `" + shape[4] + "` links"
        )
        flipped = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: shape[0] + "_flip")
        assert renamed(said: flipped.err, from: "apick", to: "picker") == one.err

        # `ab`: Apple's linker loads `libab`'s one file for `a_only` and takes
        # its `pick` in both orders, while `libbs`'s own call reaches it; the
        # probe that loads every file whole names both.
        if shape[0] != "weak"
            assert core_strings.contains(
                s: one.err,
                substr: opening + "is also defined by library `" + shape[3] + "`, which module `" + shape[4] + "` links" + whole
            )
            assert definitions_named(one.err) == 2

    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a library defining a name the runtime calls or defines is refused, unless a group of it binds that name (defects 2363 and 2378)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/runtime2363"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "r", sources: [Written(name: "r_only.c", text: "int r_only(void) { return 30; }\n"), Written(name: "printf_r.c", text: "int printf(const char *f, ...) { (void)f; return 0; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "hh", sources: [Written(name: "hh.c", text: "int h_only(void) { return 5; }\nvoid hero_print_end(void) { }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "myalloc", sources: [Written(name: "myalloc.c", text: ALLOCATOR)], shared: false).is_err()
    served = "    function myalloc_served() -> i64\n\nfunction served() -> i64\n    return myalloc_served()\n"
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "r.h", text: "int r_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "hh.h", text: "int h_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "myalloc.h", text: "#include <stddef.h>\nvoid *malloc(size_t n);\nvoid *calloc(size_t c, size_t n);\nvoid *realloc(void *p, size_t n);\nvoid free(void *p);\nlong myalloc_served(void);\n"), Written(name: "loud.hero", text: other_module(header: "r.h", library: "r", bound: "r_only")), Written(name: "hh_lib.hero", text: other_module(header: "hh.h", library: "hh", bound: "h_only")), Written(name: "runtime_name.hero", text: "use loud\n\nfunction main()\n    print(loud.only())\n    print(7)\n"), Written(name: "hero_name.hero", text: "use hh_lib\n\nfunction main()\n    print(hh_lib.only())\n"), Written(name: "counting.hero", text: "extern \"myalloc.h\" link \"myalloc\"\n" + served), Written(name: "counting2.hero", text: "extern \"myalloc.h\" link \"myalloc\"\n    function malloc(n: u64) -> ptr\n" + served), Written(name: "counting4.hero", text: "extern \"myalloc.h\" link \"myalloc\"\n    function malloc(n: u64) -> ptr\n    function calloc(c: u64, n: u64) -> ptr\n    function realloc(p: ptr, n: u64) -> ptr\n    function free(p: ptr)\n" + served), Written(name: "alloc_main.hero", text: counted(module: "counting")), Written(name: "alloc_bound.hero", text: counted(module: "counting2")), Written(name: "alloc_all.hero", text: counted(module: "counting4"))]).is_err()
    told = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "runtime_name")
    assert refused_at(
        told,
        dir: dir,
        module: "loud",
        headline: "library `r`, which module `loud` links, defines `printf`, of the C library, which the program's own runtime calls: the runtime's calls would reach library `r`'s",
        program: "runtime_name"
    )

    # Defect 2378: the runtime's own name, which the link refused as a
    # duplicate and the compiler told as its own `internal error` at exit 2.
    named = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "hero_name")
    assert refused_at(
        named,
        dir: dir,
        module: "hh_lib",
        headline: "library `hh`, which module `hh_lib` links, defines `hero_print_end`, a name of the program's own runtime, and a program holds one `hero_print_end`",
        program: "hero_name"
    )
    assert core_strings.contains(
        s: named.err,
        substr: "  note: `hero_print_end` is the runtime's own: rename it where library `hh` is built, or link a library that does not define it\n"
    )

    # An allocator replaces `malloc` on purpose only where its group binds
    # every name of the C library it defines that the runtime calls.
    for shape in [["alloc_main", "counting", "`calloc`, `free`, `malloc`"], ["alloc_bound", "counting2", "`calloc`, `free`"]]
        alloc = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: shape[0])
        assert refused_at(
            alloc,
            dir: dir,
            module: shape[1],
            headline: "library `myalloc`, which module `" + shape[1] + "` links, defines " + shape[2] + ", of the C library, which the program's own runtime calls",
            program: shape[0]
        )

    every_bound = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "alloc_all")
    assert every_bound.err == "wrote " + dir + "/alloc_all.out\n"
    assert every_bound.code == 0
    assert ran_out(scratch: scratch, binary: dir + "/alloc_all.out") == "100\ntrue\n"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# A program printing a hundred strings' count and whether `module`'s
# allocator served any of them.
function counted(module: str) -> str
    return "use " + module + "\n\nfunction main()\n    items: [str] @= []\n\n    for i in range(from: 0, to: 100)\n        items @ items.push(i.to_str() + \"x\")\n\n    print(items.len())\n    print(" + module + ".served() > 0)\n"

# What `binary` printed on stdout, run with no word.
function ran_out(scratch: str, binary: str) -> str
    return shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: binary, words: [binary]).must().out

# A counting allocator replacing the C library's four on purpose, each block
# served by the system's own (the sitting's `myalloc.c`).
constant ALLOCATOR: str
    "#include <stddef.h>\n#ifdef __APPLE__\n#include <malloc/malloc.h>\nstatic void *sys_malloc(size_t n) { return malloc_zone_malloc(malloc_default_zone(), n); }\nstatic void *sys_calloc(size_t c, size_t n) { return malloc_zone_calloc(malloc_default_zone(), c, n); }\nstatic void *sys_realloc(void *p, size_t n) {\n  if (!p) return sys_malloc(n);\n  malloc_zone_t *z = malloc_zone_from_ptr(p);\n  return malloc_zone_realloc(z ? z : malloc_default_zone(), p, n);\n}\nstatic void sys_free(void *p) { if (p) { malloc_zone_t *z = malloc_zone_from_ptr(p); if (z) malloc_zone_free(z, p); } }\n#else\nextern void *__libc_malloc(size_t); extern void *__libc_calloc(size_t, size_t);\nextern void *__libc_realloc(void *, size_t); extern void __libc_free(void *);\nstatic void *sys_malloc(size_t n) { return __libc_malloc(n); }\nstatic void *sys_calloc(size_t c, size_t n) { return __libc_calloc(c, n); }\nstatic void *sys_realloc(void *p, size_t n) { return __libc_realloc(p, n); }\nstatic void sys_free(void *p) { __libc_free(p); }\n#endif\nstatic long served;\nvoid *malloc(size_t n) { served++; return sys_malloc(n); }\nvoid *calloc(size_t c, size_t n) { served++; return sys_calloc(c, n); }\nvoid *realloc(void *p, size_t n) { served++; return sys_realloc(p, n); }\nvoid free(void *p) { sys_free(p); }\nlong myalloc_served(void) { return served; }\n"

test "the probes' answer kept for a warm build is asked again once a library the link read is rebuilt (defect 2363)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/kept2363"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    pick_a = Written(name: "pick_a.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 1; }\n")
    a_only = Written(name: "a_only.c", text: "int a_only(void) { return 10; }\n")
    b_only = Written(name: "b_only.c", text: "int b_only(void) { return 20; }\n")
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "a", sources: [pick_a, a_only], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "b", sources: [b_only], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "a.h", text: "int pick(void);\nint a_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "b.h", text: "int b_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "apick.hero", text: PICKER), Written(name: "extra.hero", text: other_module(header: "b.h", library: "b", bound: "b_only")), Written(name: "main.hero", text: both_used(first: "apick", second: "extra", group: "apick", other: "extra"))]).is_err()

    # Built twice, the second warm: the libraries stand `a` first, so the
    # program's own link takes `liba`'s `pick`, and `libb` defines no other.
    first = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "main")
    assert first.err == "wrote " + dir + "/main.out\n" && first.code == 0
    assert ran_out(scratch: scratch, binary: dir + "/main.out") == "1\n20\n"
    warm = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "main")
    assert warm.err == "wrote " + dir + "/main.out\n" && warm.code == 0
    assert ran_out(scratch: scratch, binary: dir + "/main.out") == "1\n20\n"

    # `libb` rebuilt with a `pick` of its own: the program's link still takes
    # `liba`'s, and only the probe in the other order reaches `libb`'s, so an
    # answer kept by the link's words alone would build it.
    assert !shell.remove_tree(dir + "/libb.a").is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "b", sources: [Written(name: "pick_b.c", text: "int pick(void) { return 2; }\n"), b_only], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !shell.remove_tree(dir + "/main.out").is_err()
    rebuilt = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "main")
    assert refused_at(
        rebuilt,
        dir: dir,
        module: "apick",
        headline: "`pick`, bound here from library `a`, is also defined by library `b`, which module `extra` links",
        program: "main"
    )
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# PANEL 239's R3, a known cost pinned (defect 2375): a name no group binds and
# the runtime does not call, `base_helper`, defined by two libraries the
# program links, `libbase`'s returning 40 and `libother`'s 99, and needed by a
# third, `libtop`, whose `top_value` adds one to it, reaches whichever the
# link takes, and nothing is told: `100` where `libbase`'s would give `41`, on
# this Mac and in the Linux arm64 container (2026-11-09, the sitting's
# `intern_sorted`, the ffi-pragmatist's prediction `100 2 7`). It is not
# refused because refusing every such duplicate refuses correct static
# programs whose libraries carry identical copies on purpose: static FFmpeg,
# 40 names defined in two of `libavcodec.a`, `libavformat.a` and
# `libavutil.a`, and static X11, `xreallocarray` in `libX11.a` and
# `libXext.a`, as the sitting measured, and no symbol tells an identical copy
# from two different ones. Kept so by the author's answer of 2026-11-09 to
# decision 2374's second question (defect 2375). This case pins the silence:
# the day a route tells the shape, it moves here, and is read.
test "a name no group binds, defined by two libraries and needed by a third, stays silent, a known cost (defect 2375)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/intern2375"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "top", sources: [Written(name: "top.c", text: "int base_helper(void);\nint top_value(void) { return base_helper() + 1; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "base", sources: [Written(name: "base_helper.c", text: "int base_helper(void) { return 40; }\n"), Written(name: "base_other.c", text: "int base_other(void) { return 2; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !c_library(scratch: scratch, dir: dir, library: "other", sources: [Written(name: "other.c", text: "int base_helper(void) { return 99; }\nint other_only(void) { return 7; }\n")], shared: false).is_err()
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "top.h", text: "int top_value(void);\n"), Written(name: "base.h", text: "int base_other(void);\n"), Written(name: "other.h", text: "int other_only(void);\n"), Written(name: "top_lib.hero", text: "extern \"top.h\" link \"top\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n\nfunction value() -> i32\n    return top_value()\n"), Written(name: "base_lib.hero", text: other_module(header: "base.h", library: "base", bound: "base_other")), Written(name: "other_lib.hero", text: other_module(header: "other.h", library: "other", bound: "other_only")), Written(name: "intern.hero", text: "use other_lib\nuse base_lib\nuse top_lib\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_lib.value())\n    print(base_lib.only())\n    print(other_lib.only())\n")]).is_err()
    kept = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "intern")
    assert kept.err == "wrote " + dir + "/intern.out\n" && kept.code == 0
    assert ran_out(scratch: scratch, binary: dir + "/intern.out") == "100\n2\n7\n"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "two libraries of the platform a program names, each its group's, build as they did (panel 239)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/platform2363"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()

    # glibc defines `ldexp` in `libc.so.6` and `libm.so.6` both, and
    # `pthread_self` in `libc.so.6` behind `libpthread.a`; Apple's in
    # `libsystem_m` and `libsystem_pthread`: libraries the program does not
    # name, never judged. `pthread_t` is a number on glibc and a pointer on
    # Darwin, so each asks its own pair.
    threads: str @= "extern \"pthread.h\" link \"pthread\"\n    function pthread_self() -> u64\n    function pthread_equal(a: u64, b: u64) -> i32\n\nfunction level() -> i32\n    me = pthread_self()\n    return pthread_equal(a: me, b: me)\n"
    wanted: str @= "1\n16.0\n"

    if darwin(scratch)
        threads @ "extern \"pthread.h\" link \"pthread\"\n    function pthread_getconcurrency() -> i32\n    function pthread_setconcurrency(level: i32) -> i32\n\nfunction level() -> i32\n    _ = pthread_setconcurrency(level: 0)\n    return pthread_getconcurrency()\n"
        wanted @ "0\n16.0\n"
    assert !wrote(dir: dir, files: [Written(name: "threads.hero", text: threads), Written(name: "mathy.hero", text: "extern \"math.h\" link \"m\"\n    function sqrt(x: f64) -> f64\n    function ldexp(x: f64, e: i32) -> f64\n\nfunction scaled() -> f64\n    return ldexp(x: sqrt(x: 4.0), e: 3)\n"), Written(name: "pair.hero", text: "use mathy\nuse threads\n\nfunction main()\n    print(threads.level())\n    print(mathy.scaled())\n")]).is_err()
    pair = built_against(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, dir: dir, program: "pair")
    assert pair.err == "wrote " + dir + "/pair.out\n"
    assert pair.code == 0
    assert ran_out(scratch: scratch, binary: dir + "/pair.out") == wanted
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 2365, asked of the compiler itself: a `link` group's library that
# needs a name nothing on the link line defines, a `libtop2365.a` whose
# `top_value` calls a `base_helper` no library of the program has, is told
# at the group's `link` string, `ffi_missing_link` at exit 1, the library and
# the name named, where every platform's linker ended the build at exit 2 as
# *internal error: linking failed* (panel 238's `only_top`, measured on this
# Mac and in the `heroes-linux-arm64` container, 2026-11-08); and the same
# library carried by a package's answer is told at the package's string. The
# archive is made by the machine's own `ar`, which stands beside GNU ld and
# ld64 alike; a machine that runs no script, Windows, is not asked.
test "a name a linked library needs and nothing defines is told at its group or its package, never as an internal error (defect 2365)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/needs2365"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/top.c", text: "int base_helper(void);\nint top_value(void);\nint top_value(void) { return base_helper() + 1; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/top2365.h", text: "int top_value(void);\n").is_err()
    made = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: "clang",
        words: ["clang", "-c", "-fPIC", dir + "/top.c", "-o", dir + "/top.o"]
    ).must()
    assert made.err == ""
    assert made.code == 0
    archived = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: "ar",
        words: ["ar", "rcs", dir + "/libtop2365.a", dir + "/top.o"]
    ).must()
    assert archived.code == 0
    linked = dir + "/linked.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: linked, text: "extern \"top2365.h\" link \"top2365\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value())\n").is_err()
    said = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "build", linked, "--library", dir, "-o", linked + ".out"]
    ).must()
    assert core_strings.contains(
        s: said.err,
        substr: "error[ffi_missing_link]: the library `top2365` needs `base_helper`, and nothing on the link line defines it\n  at " + linked + ":1:25\n"
    )
    assert core_strings.contains(
        s: said.err,
        substr: "  note: `libtop2365.a` refers to `base_helper` without defining it, and a library is linked only where a group names it: give this program a group for the library that defines it as well, `extern \"<its header>\" link \"<library>\"`, holding a function its header declares\n"
    )
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: said.err, substr: "internal error")
    assert said.code == 1
    assert !shell.exists(linked + ".out")

    # The same library carried by a package's answer, told at its string.
    bin = dir + "/bin"
    assert !answering(dir: bin, answer: "-I" + dir + " -L" + dir + " -ltop2365\n").is_err()
    path = dir + "/answered.hero"
    told = built_said(
        scratch: scratch,
        compiler: compiler,
        path: path,
        source: "extern \"top2365.h\" pkgconfig \"ptop2365\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value())\n",
        bin: bin,
        alone: false
    ).must()
    assert core_strings.contains(
        s: told,
        substr: "error[ffi_missing_link]: the library `top2365`, which `pkg-config` answered for `ptop2365`, needs `base_helper`, and nothing on the link line defines it\n  at " + path + ":1:30\n"
    )
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: told, substr: "internal error")
    assert !shell.exists(path + ".out")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 2372, asked of the compiler itself: a group binding `top_value`
# from a header whose `static inline` body calls `base_helper`, the group
# linking a `libtop2372.a` that defines nothing the program calls, is told at
# the binding, `ffi_missing_link` at exit 1, the header, the function and the
# name named, at `-O0` and at `-O2`, where the linker names the header's
# function as referring at the first and the compiler's own caller beside it
# at the second (measured on this Mac, ld64-27106.1, and in the
# `heroes-linux-arm64` container, GNU ld 2.44, 2026-11-09); every platform
# ended the build at exit 2 as *internal error: linking failed*. And the name
# defined by a library the program does link builds and runs. The archives
# are made by the machine's own `ar`; a machine that runs no script, Windows,
# is not asked.
test "a header's inline body calling a name nothing on the link line defines is told at its binding at both levels (defect 2372)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/needs2372"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/top.c", text: "int top_other(void) { return 1; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/base.c", text: "int base_helper(void) { return 40; }\nint base_other(void) { return 2; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/fast2372.h", text: "int base_helper(void);\nstatic inline int top_value(void) { return base_helper() + 1; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/base2372.h", text: "int base_other(void);\n").is_err()

    for name in ["top", "base"]
        made = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "clang",
            words: ["clang", "-c", "-fPIC", dir + "/" + name + ".c", "-o", dir + "/" + name + ".o"]
        ).must()
        assert made.err == "" && made.code == 0
        archived = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "ar",
            words: ["ar", "rcs", dir + "/lib" + name + "2372.a", dir + "/" + name + ".o"]
        ).must()
        assert archived.code == 0

    path = dir + "/inline.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: path, text: "extern \"fast2372.h\" link \"top2372\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value())\n").is_err()

    for level in ["-O0", "-O2"]
        said = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: compiler,
            words: [compiler, "build", level, path, "--library", dir, "-o", path + ".out"]
        ).must()
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: said.err,
            substr: "error[ffi_missing_link]: `top_value` is defined inline in `fast2372.h` line 2, and its body calls `base_helper`, which nothing on the link line defines\n  at " + path + ":2:5\n"
        )
        assert core_strings.contains(
            s: said.err,
            substr: "  note: this program needs one definition of `base_helper` on its link line: the body of a function a header defines is compiled into this program's own object wherever the program reaches it, at `-O0` and `-O2` alike; a definition comes from a library, linked only where a group names it, or from a header that defines it, compiled into every module whose group names that header; give this program a group for that library, `extern \"<its header>\" link \"<library>\"`, holding a function its header declares; or have one module's group, and no other's, name a header that defines it\n"
        )
        assert !core_strings.contains(s: said.err, substr: "internal error")
        assert said.code == 1
        assert !shell.exists(path + ".out")

    # The name defined by a library the program links: it builds and runs.
    linked = dir + "/linked.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: linked, text: "extern \"fast2372.h\" link \"top2372\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n\nextern \"base2372.h\" link \"base2372\"\n    function base_other() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value() + base_other())\n").is_err()
    built = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "build", "-O2", linked, "--library", dir, "-o", linked + ".out"]
    ).must()
    assert built.code == 0
    ran = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: linked + ".out", words: [linked + ".out"]).must()
    assert ran.out == "43\n"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# DEFECT 2381, asked of the compiler itself: one link missing a name a
# linked library needs, `libtop2381.a`'s `top_value` calling `base_helper`,
# and a name a header's inline body needs, `top_fast` calling `base_two`, was
# told by neither defect 2365's reader nor 2372's, each judging the whole
# link, *internal error: linking failed* at exit 2 (measured on this Mac,
# ld64-27106.1, and in the `heroes-linux-arm64` container, GNU ld 2.44,
# 2026-11-09). Each name is now told by its reader, at `-O0` and `-O2`, in
# the program's order; three names over the two readers likewise; and a name
# neither answers, one of the compiler's own forms a header's body calls,
# leaves the link the compiler's, exit 2. The archives are made by the
# machine's own `ar`; a machine that runs no script, Windows, is not asked.
test "each name a refused link reports is told by the reader whose question it answers, or the link is the compiler's (defect 2381)"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    compiler = shell.some_compiler().must()
    dir = scratch + "/needs2381"
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/top.c", text: "int base_helper(void);\nint top_value(void) { return base_helper() + 1; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/other.c", text: "int base_three(void);\nint other_value(void) { return base_three() + 3; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/mixed2381.h", text: "int top_value(void);\nint base_two(void);\nint hero_gone2381(void);\nstatic inline int top_fast(void) { return base_two() + 1; }\nstatic inline int top_gone(void) { return hero_gone2381() + 1; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/other2381.h", text: "int other_value(void);\n").is_err()

    for name in ["top", "other"]
        made = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "clang",
            words: ["clang", "-c", "-fPIC", dir + "/" + name + ".c", "-o", dir + "/" + name + ".o"]
        ).must()
        assert made.err == "" && made.code == 0
        archived = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: "ar",
            words: ["ar", "rcs", dir + "/lib" + name + "2381.a", dir + "/" + name + ".o"]
        ).must()
        assert archived.code == 0

    library = "error[ffi_missing_link]: the library `top2381` needs `base_helper`, and nothing on the link line defines it\n  at "
    header = "error[ffi_missing_link]: `top_fast` is defined inline in `mixed2381.h` line 4, and its body calls `base_two`, which nothing on the link line defines\n  at "
    path = dir + "/mixed.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: path, text: "extern \"mixed2381.h\" link \"top2381\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n    function top_fast() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value() + top_fast())\n").is_err()

    for level in ["-O0", "-O2"]
        said = shell.run(
            scratch: scratch,
            program: compiler,
            words: [compiler, "build", level, path, "--library", dir, "-o", path + ".out"]
        ).must()
        assert core_strings.contains(s: said.err, substr: library + path + ":1:27\n")
        assert core_strings.contains(s: said.err, substr: header + path + ":3:5\n")
        assert !core_strings.contains(s: said.err, substr: "internal error")
        assert said.code == 1
        assert !shell.exists(path + ".out")

    # Three names over the two readers, each told, in the program's order.
    three = dir + "/three.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: three, text: "extern \"mixed2381.h\" link \"top2381\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n    function top_fast() -> i32\n\nextern \"other2381.h\" link \"other2381\"\n    function other_value() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value() + top_fast() + other_value())\n").is_err()
    told = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "build", three, "--library", dir, "-o", three + ".out"]
    ).must()
    first = strings.index_of(text: told.err, needle: library + three + ":1:27\n").default(-1)
    second = strings.index_of(text: told.err, needle: header + three + ":3:5\n").default(-1)
    third = strings.index_of(text: told.err, needle: "error[ffi_missing_link]: the library `other2381` needs `base_three`, and nothing on the link line defines it\n  at " + three + ":5:27\n").default(
        -1
    )
    assert first >= 0 && second > first && third > second
    assert told.code == 1

    # A name neither reader answers beside two each answers: the compiler's.
    neither = dir + "/neither.hero"
    assert !write_file(path: neither, text: "extern \"mixed2381.h\" link \"top2381\"\n    function top_value() -> i32\n    function top_fast() -> i32\n    function top_gone() -> i32\n\nfunction main()\n    print(top_value() + top_fast() + top_gone())\n").is_err()
    left = shell.run(
        scratch: scratch,
        program: compiler,
        words: [compiler, "build", neither, "--library", dir, "-o", neither + ".out"]
    ).must()
    assert core_strings.contains(s: left.err, substr: "internal error: linking failed")
    assert !core_strings.contains(s: left.err, substr: "error[ffi_missing_link]")
    assert left.code == 2
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# The two answers are pkgconf's own, recorded by panel 188's ffi-pragmatist on
# this Mac (3.0.7) and in the Linux image (1.8.1), 2026-10-03, for a package
# named `zz9`.
test "pkg-config's answer becomes the compiler's notes, in either pkgconf's words"
    mac = "Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPerhaps you should add the directory containing `zz9.pc'\nto the PKG_CONFIG_PATH environment variable\nPackage 'zz9' not found\n"
    assert notes_of(mac) == ["  note: pkg-config said: Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.", "  note: Perhaps you should add the directory containing `zz9.pc'", "  note: to the PKG_CONFIG_PATH environment variable", "  note: Package 'zz9' not found"]
    linux = "Package zz9 was not found in the pkg-config search path.\nPerhaps you should add the directory containing `zz9.pc'\nto the PKG_CONFIG_PATH environment variable\nPackage 'zz9', required by 'virtual:world', not found\n"
    assert notes_of(linux)[3] == "  note: Package 'zz9', required by 'virtual:world', not found"

    # `\r\n` is one line end, an empty line no note.
    assert notes_of("one\r\n\r\n  two  \n") == ["  note: pkg-config said: one", "  note: two"]

    # The LAST `\r\n` is a line end too, read before the trim as the compiler
    # reads it since defect 285; until defect 328 the trim came first here,
    # the pair lost its `\n`, and this answer was refused.
    assert notes_of("one\r\ntwo\r\n") == ["  note: pkg-config said: one", "  note: two"]

    # Nothing said, or nothing but blanks and line ends, is the note that
    # says so (defect 326); it was `pkg-config said:` over nothing here.
    assert notes_of("") == ["  note: pkg-config gave no reason"]
    assert notes_of("  \r\n\n") == ["  note: pkg-config gave no reason"]

    # What the compiler writes by its code is written so here: a tab, a lone
    # return, and DEL and a C1 control, whose bytes no literal of this
    # language spells, so `printf` writes them (POSIX, as the script cases).
    assert notes_of("a\tb") == ["  note: pkg-config said: a<U+0009>b"]
    assert notes_of("a\rb\r") == ["  note: pkg-config said: a<U+000D>b<U+000D>"]
    assert notes_of("café") == ["  note: pkg-config said: café"]

    if shell.can_run_scripts()
        controls = shell.run(
            scratch: "build/harness-selftest",
            program: "/bin/sh",
            words: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf 'a\\177b\\na\\302\\205b'"]
        )
        assert !controls.is_err()
        two = strings.lines(controls.must().out)
        assert two.len() == 2
        assert notes_of(two[0]) == ["  note: pkg-config said: a<U+007F>b"]
        assert notes_of(two[1]) == ["  note: pkg-config said: a<U+0085>b"]

    # The way out closes them, the installed headline's naming what pkg-config
    # said it lacks only where it said anything.
    assert quoted_notes(answer: "x\n", kind: "package") == [NOTE + SAID + "x", NOTE + NOT_INSTALLED_WAY]
    assert quoted_notes(answer: "x\n", kind: "installed") == [NOTE + SAID + "x", NOTE + INSTALLED_WAY]
    assert quoted_notes(answer: " \n", kind: "installed") == [NOTE + NO_REASON, NOTE + SILENT_WAY]

test "an expectation's quotation is this machine's answer, and no other line moves"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    package = "heroes-absence-a-package-no-machine-has"
    head = package_said(package: package, at: "x.hero:1:26")
    last = NOTE + NOT_INSTALLED_WAY + "\n"
    foreign = head + "  note: pkg-config said: words another machine wrote\n  note: and a second line of them\n" + last
    got = quoted_here(scratch: scratch, expected: foreign)
    here = quotation(scratch: scratch, package: package, kind: "package")

    if here.is_err()
        # No `pkg-config` here, or one that knows the package: as written.
        assert got == foreign
    else
        assert got == head + here.must().join("\n") + "\n"
        assert quoted_here(scratch: scratch, expected: got) == got

        # A quotation that said nothing is a frame too, its one note the
        # opening and the way out the close (defect 326).
        silent = head + NOTE + NO_REASON + "\n" + last
        assert quoted_here(scratch: scratch, expected: silent) == got

    # No frame, no change: a header's message, and a package's message whose
    # closing note is missing.
    header = "error[ffi_missing_header]: `gone.h` is not on this machine's include path\n"
    assert quoted_here(scratch: scratch, expected: header) == header
    unclosed = head + "  note: pkg-config said: words\n"
    assert quoted_here(scratch: scratch, expected: unclosed) == unclosed
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a case's directory is where clang looks beside it"
    assert directory_of("tests/golden/unsupported/x.hero") == "tests/golden/unsupported"
    assert directory_of("x.hero") == "."

test "a program's witness reads the file it was given, its modules, and what the compiler blames beside them"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    tree = scratch + "/absence-sources"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(tree).is_err()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(tree + "/db").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: tree + "/main.hero", text: "use db/store\n\nfunction main()\n    print(1)\n").is_err()
    store = "extern \"" + NOWHERE + "\"\n    function seven() -> i32  #~ ffi_missing_header\n"
    assert !write_file(path: tree + "/db/store.hero", text: store).is_err()
    blamed = absences(header_said(path: tree + "/db/store.hero", source: store, header: NOWHERE).must())
    read = read_sources(path: tree + "/main.hero", modules: [], named: blamed)
    assert !read.is_err()
    assert read.must().len() == 2
    assert read.must()[1].path == tree + "/db/store.hero"
    assert binds_of(read.must()).headers == [NOWHERE]

    # The module's own mark keys by its file, which is how the place pins it.
    assert marks_of(read.must()) == [tree + "/db/store.hero:2:ffi_missing_header"]
    assert blamed[0].line == 2

    # A file outside the program's directory is never read on the compiler's
    # word, and one that cannot be read fails the question.
    outside = absences(missing_header_said(header: NOWHERE, tail: "", at: "elsewhere/x.hero:1:1"))
    assert read_sources(path: tree + "/main.hero", modules: [], named: outside).must().len() == 1
    gone = absences(missing_header_said(header: NOWHERE, tail: "", at: tree + "/db/gone.hero:1:1"))
    assert read_sources(path: tree + "/main.hero", modules: [], named: gone).is_err()
    assert bound_by(path: "p/main.hero", count: 1) == "p/main.hero binds"
    assert bound_by(path: "p/main.hero", count: 2) == "p/main.hero and the one other module read with it bind"
    assert bound_by(path: "p/main.hero", count: 4) == "p/main.hero and the 3 other modules read with it bind"
    assert inside(dir: ".", file: "x.hero")
    assert !inside(dir: ".", file: "a/x.hero")
    assert inside(dir: "a", file: "a/b/x.hero")
    assert !inside(dir: "a", file: "ab/x.hero")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

# Defect 243 at the harness's second reader (2026-10-04): a `HEROES_RUNTIME`
# that is not UTF-8 refuses the search, so no absence is believed, where it
# read as unset and clang searched `runtime` in its place. The value itself is
# read through a child's environment in `shell.hero`'s test of the same day.
test "fixedbugs: a runtime hint that cannot be read refuses the search, and an unset one searches runtime"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    nothing: [str] = []
    none = Binds(headers: nothing, packages: nothing)
    unread: str? = fail(code: "unasked", msg: "HEROES_RUNTIME is set to `rt<0xE9>`, which is not UTF-8")
    unasked = searched(scratch: scratch, dir: ".", bound: none, hint: unread)
    assert unasked.is_err()
    assert message_of_words(unasked) == "HEROES_RUNTIME is set to `rt<0xE9>`, which is not UTF-8"
    unset: str? = ok("")
    assert searched(scratch: scratch, dir: "cases", bound: none, hint: unset).must() == ["-I", "runtime", "-I", "cases"]
    named: str? = ok("elsewhere/rt")
    assert searched(scratch: scratch, dir: "cases", bound: none, hint: named).must() == ["-I", "elsewhere/rt", "-I", "cases"]
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a refused skip says why only where the compiler named a library"
    nothing: str? = fail(code: "none", msg: "the compiler names nothing this machine lacks")
    assert refused(nothing) == ""
    refuted: str? = fail(code: "refuted", msg: "clang finds `stdio.h`")
    assert refused(refuted) == "clang finds `stdio.h`"
    skipped: str? = ok("this machine lacks it")
    assert refused(skipped) == ""

# **The premise the driver's sentence about `pkg-config` rests on**, owed its
# test (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`): that sentence names a package and no
# file, so its witness finds the `pkgconfig` group only in the files a suite
# hands the compiler, a program's root, and every root holds a `main`. A
# `pkgconfig` group moved into a module would read as bound by nothing on a
# machine with no `pkg-config`, and its case would go red there rather than
# be skipped; this fires first, on every machine.
test "every package group sits in a program's root, where the pkg-config witness reads"
    roots: [str] = ["examples", "tests/golden"]
    nothing: [str] = []
    seen: i64 @= 0

    for root in roots
        listed_files = shell.list_tree(scratch: "build/harness-selftest", dir: root, skip: nothing)
        assert !listed_files.is_err()

        for file in listed_files.must()
            if !core_strings.has_suffix(s: file, suffix: ".hero")
                continue
            text = read_file(file)

            if text.is_err()
                continue

            if binds(text.must()).packages.len() > 0
                seen @ seen + 1
                assert core_strings.contains(s: text.must(), substr: "function main")

    # Nineteen such roots on 2026-10-04, counted by a grep for the same lines:
    # `examples/raylib` and `examples/sdl`, eight `check/` cases, five `run/`
    # and four `unsupported/`. A walk that finds fewer is not reaching the tree.
    assert seen >= 19

# What the compiler writes on stderr building `source`, written at `path`: its
# PATH as it is where `bin` is "", else `bin` before it, or `bin` alone where
# `alone`. POSIX, as every script case here: a shell sets the PATH.
function built_said(scratch: str, compiler: str, path: str, source: str, bin: str, alone: bool) -> str?
    write_file(path: path, text: source)?
    out = path + ".out"

    if bin.len() == 0
        direct = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "build", path, "-o", out])?
        return ok(direct.err)
    path_set: str @= "\"$PWD/$3:$PATH\""

    if alone
        path_set @ "\"$PWD/$3\""
    script = "PATH=" + path_set + " exec \"$0\" build \"$1\" -o \"$2\""
    ran = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "/bin/sh", words: ["/bin/sh", "-c", script, compiler, path, out, bin])?
    return ok(ran.err)

# A stand-in `pkg-config` in `dir`: it reads no package installed, or every
# one where `installed`, has no search path of its own, and refuses every
# package's flags with `answer` on stderr, as the compiler asks each
# (`selfhost/cli/libraries.hero`, `selfhost/cli/pkgconfig_answer.hero`).
function stand_in(dir: str, answer: str, installed: bool) -> ()?
    shell.mkdir_all(dir)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/answer.txt", text: answer)?
    reads: str @= "exit 1"

    if installed
        reads @ "echo 1.0; exit 0"
    tool = dir + "/pkg-config"
    write_file(path: tool, text: "#!/bin/sh\ncase \"$1\" in\n    --version) echo 0.29.2; exit 0 ;;\n    --variable=pc_path) exit 0 ;;\n    --cflags) cat \"" + dir + "/answer.txt\" 1>&2; exit 1 ;;\n    --modversion|--maximum-traverse-depth=1) " + reads + " ;;\nesac\nexit 1\n")?
    made = shell.run(scratch: dir, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", tool])?

    if made.code != 0
        return fail(code: "cannot_run", msg: "cannot make " + tool + " executable")
    return ok()

# A stand-in `pkg-config` in `dir` that ANSWERS every package's flags with
# `answer` on stdout, where `stand_in` refuses them: the compiler asks
# `--cflags --libs` once and reads the one reply; it reads every package
# installed, as `stand_in` does where `installed`.
function answering(dir: str, answer: str) -> ()?
    shell.mkdir_all(dir)?
    write_file(path: dir + "/answer.txt", text: answer)?
    tool = dir + "/pkg-config"
    write_file(path: tool, text: "#!/bin/sh\ncase \"$1\" in\n    --version) echo 0.29.2; exit 0 ;;\n    --variable=pc_path) exit 0 ;;\n    --cflags) cat \"" + dir + "/answer.txt\"; exit 0 ;;\n    --modversion|--maximum-traverse-depth=1) echo 1.0; exit 0 ;;\nesac\nexit 1\n")?
    made = shell.run(scratch: dir, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", tool])?

    if made.code != 0
        return fail(code: "cannot_run", msg: "cannot make " + tool + " executable")
    return ok()

# The notes of a compiler's message, as the renderer writes them.
function notes_in(said: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for row in strings.lines(said)
        if core_strings.has_prefix(s: row, prefix: NOTE)
            out @ out.push(row)

    return out

# An absence with no code and no place, for the tests that ask what a case
# binds or what the machine lacks.
function an(kind: str, name: str, owner: str) -> Absence
    return Absence(kind: kind, name: name, owner: owner, code: "", file: "", line: 0)
